{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war","id":1077627,"title":"How could the Tremere manage to \"win\" the First Massasa War?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-04-13T04:30:47-07:00","id":1077627,"title":"How could the Tremere manage to \"win\" the First Massasa War?","content":"They made many powerful enemies among kindred. <br /> <br /> They were vastly outnumbered.<br /> <br /> They were &quot;new&quot; in their undead status.<br /> <br /> Their thaumaturgy was in its infancy.<br /> <br /> They stood alone against the single most powerful supernatural organization on the world, which accumulated thousands of years worth of power and knowledge. Which supposedly could take every other supernatural on its own term. If do you wish to know how mighty and glorious high medieval era OoH, see Ars Magica. <br /> <br /> And they massacred their former brethren. In literal thousands. At the end of the First Massasa War, there were less than 100 Awakened population among <i>entire Order</i>. <br /> <br /> How this feat was possible? Just another Tremere wank? Another insult on injury to the Order?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11780-posthumanity","name":"Posthumanity","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T04:34:16-07:00","id":1077630,"title":"","content":"Because it's a lot easier to turn a human into a vampire then it is to awaken one into a mage.....<br /> <br /> Also reference this thread<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere\"> Having a hard time buying into the Tremere - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere\"> http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> First off, I'm NOT JUST talking about a group of mages actually turning into vampires. Althoough it seems unlikely that the Tremere would NOT test the magic elixir </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T06:02:52-07:00","id":1077671,"title":"","content":"I think the short answer would be:<br /> <br /> The kindred of the time didn't really know how to handle them.<br /> They had powers no one had ever seen before and were actually likely preparing for the war before anyone knew what the hell was going on.<br /> They could make for up the numbers difference much better than mages and, using gargoyles, better than most vampire groups.<br /> According to a thorough reading of the texts and some extrapolation, Thaumaturgy was well developed (up to 5 dots) by the time they could make gargoyles.<br /> And by the look of it, they had a century to plan for the war and likely specifically planned against the Order of Hermes as their primary enemy.<br /> <br /> I'd say it's a bit like asking how the heck Japan conquered China and most of the pacific in WW2. They were primarily using medieval weapons and flint lock rifles not so long ago and didn't seem to know what they were doing before that time. Or how did any tiny state or group defeat any much larger, apparently better armed force. It's not a common occurrence but it does happen.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T06:22:23-07:00","id":1077682,"title":"","content":"Possibly it's just my misunderstanding of canon, but I never got the impression that the Tremere &quot;won&quot; the Massasa War so much as they ended up in kind of a stalemate before getting distracted by other concerns (i.e. the Order of Reason and the Anarch War).<br /> <br /> The Order of Hermes was very powerful at the time, but they were also always very divided, with as much fighting between houses as directed outward. There's a lot of scheming and backstabbing. Tremere, on the other hand, was a single House banded together. The Tremere knew the weak points of the Order as a full body, and had a lot of time to prepare how they would attack.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T06:31:07-07:00","id":1077689,"title":"","content":"I don't think anyone really won that war in a definitive sense. I expect that's why the word &quot;win&quot; up there is in quotation marks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T07:13:01-07:00","id":1077710,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1077689#post1077689\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think anyone really won that war in a definitive sense. I expect that's why the word &quot;win&quot; up there is in quotation marks.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed. According to revised OoH book, while Order's Awakened number was reduced to below 100, Tremere and its allies suffered similar casualties. However, as vampires, Tremere was able to recover much faster than its former brethren. If not Anarch war and other catastrophic events, Tremere could eradicate the Order from face of the Earth, as Order themselves acknowledge. <br /> <br /> So, in a sense, Tremere won against a vastly superior opponent dwarfing the clan in every measurable way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11780-posthumanity","name":"Posthumanity","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T07:26:26-07:00","id":1077716,"title":"","content":"If you look at the typical vampire stats for Tremere in the book about the war (forget the name), they have absolutely insane stats. Like, 5s in most spheres and zero paradox. <br /> <br /> In essence, in &quot;Canon&quot;, Vampires are far stronger then mages. If youre playing vampire, you give Mages thaumaturgy with none of the vampiric benifits, if youre playing Mage, you give vampires spheres and vampire traits. <br /> <br /> If played system vs system, then yeah, Mages woulda won. But its played Story vs Story, and in Whitewolf's vision, vampires are stronger then mages. (Which is bs but whatevs)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11894-shushu","name":"Shushu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"63"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T07:46:49-07:00","id":1077731,"title":"","content":"If vampires are that strong, why they have not already dominated supernatural world, crushed every obstacle, exterminated all enemies and ruled the earth and its &quot;kine&quot; as immortal overlord-leeches? After all, vampires are not only the strongest, but also the most numerous among supernatural denizens.<br /> <br /> During medieval, at the very apogee of the vampiric power and glory, even in Eastern Europe, in <i>Transylvania</i>, vampires lived in fear of lupines. They also feared the human mage as much as werebeasts, ghosts, fairies and demons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11780,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11780-posthumanity","user":"Posthumanity","date":". 04-13-2017, 10:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11780-posthumanity","name":"Posthumanity","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T08:15:25-07:00","id":1077746,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure how close this is to canon, but this is my interpretation: in 1197, the rest of the Order of Hermes discovers that most of the Tremere have been turned into vampires. The Tremere are expelled from the Order of Hermes, with the few non-vampiric magi of House Tremere joining other houses, primarily House Tytalus. A brief war follows, which ends in a stalemate, and the Hermetics and the Tremere basically agree to leave each other alone after that. This is the situation which exists at the start of the Second Massasa War detailed in Blood Treachery.<br /> <br /> I'm curious now; please let me know how well my version accords with White Wolf/Onyx Path canon. Thanks!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Our society is held together by the thinnest of threads: that exquisitely refined sense of tribal order.<br /> Edith Wharton, The Age of Innocence<br /> <br /> I am a huge fan of both the Trinity Universe and the classic World of Darkness.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12799-spellfire22","name":"Spellfire22","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T09:44:15-07:00","id":1077775,"title":"","content":"Sounds about right.<br /> <br /> I think the main factor regarding how the Tremere did as well as they did is really how well prepared they were for it, pretty much having well over a century to figure out what to do when they are finally found out. They probably tried to think up everything the Order could throw at them and develop countermeasures for these. The OoH book mentions the use of firestorms, living-stone monsters (gargoyles), ghouls, castle demolition, and dragons. The Order didn't really understand what it was up against and the Tremere knew exactly what they were up against.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12480,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","user":"Spacecat","date":". 04-13-2017, 12:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T11:03:17-07:00","id":1077807,"title":"","content":"You gotta figure that even the most well-attended Wizard March only mobilizes like 20% of the Order of Hermes, really. I still think the Tremere's survival is pretty miraculous given how many people they're supposed to have pissed off, but I'd say that antagonizing the Tzimisce in the seat of their power is far more threatening that torquing off the (relatively widely-distributed and non-cooperative) Order of Hermes. The Order may have some people in it that can annihilate whole chantries with meteors from space, but it's composed of the sort of people that wouldn't bother to do so just out of loyalty to the organization. The latter medieval period is supposed to be a decadent era for the Hermetics - they're magically powerful, sure, but they're also at one of their most dysfunctional points as a fellowship.<br /> <br /> (I think the &quot;less than 100&quot; number in the Revised OoH book should probably just be ignored, in any case: It's always a bad idea for WoD stuff to list numbers.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i> I attack people with giant insects both </i>on <i>and </i>off <i>the court.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1653-five-eyes","name":"Five Eyes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1653_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"395"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T12:32:09-07:00","id":1077851,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Posthumanity</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">If do you wish to know how mighty and glorious high medieval era OoH, see Ars Magica.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's interesting to note that, in Ars Magica, the Tremere seem to have lost the war as their House still stands with mortal magi after the Massa War (and not in ExMiscelanea).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Posthumanity</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">And they massacred their former brethren. In literal thousands. At the end of the First Massasa War, there were less than 100 Awakened population among <i>entire Order</i>.<br /> <br /> How this feat was possible? Just another Tremere wank? Another insult on injury to the Order?</div> </div> </div> </div>The 100 Awakened population deserves no more atention that the normal given to those &quot;censes&quot;. Mages are always hit with the &quot;rare&quot; stick (to the point of saying that in modern days there are 1 per 1.000.000 people, which would put the OoH arround 333-400 members between all Houses) so I don't know if &quot;100&quot; it's 5% of what the Order had before the war, 20%, or 75%, especially if you count the House Tremere as part of the looses.<br /> Since you get to play with the OoH in DA: Mage, which despite the recent looses, it's still one of the most powerful mage groups arround 1230 (after the Massa War had cooled down) I would say that they weren't as crippled as the Tradition book seems to imply.<br /> <br /> I think it's worth to note that the War lasted until 1230 not because the OoH was dying, but because the Church started their Inquisiton. It seems that Hermetics weren't hiding their black robes and pointy hats at the moment, because they were hit *hard* by it. Supposedly Goroatrix wasn't above infiltrating the Inquisition to send them after mages, so that helped (this may have backfired after a time. Goroatrix was accused of calling attention to vampires because of his &quot;careless manipulations&quot;. Maybe the Church doesn't need a century to figure out they're being dupped by vampires, or maybe it was just that, for Etrius, Goroatrix it's the excuse of anything bad that happens under the moon).<br /> <br /> Any case, <i>as per Players Guide of the Low Clans, p. 62. </i>the Church (and the Messianic Voices?) was thought to be a&quot;good deal more immediately threatening to the Order than the Tremere&quot;. Perhaps because the Tremere didn't want to fight against their former brethren for sentimental reasons. I want to note that the book states that, while the Tremere didn't try to stamp out their former brethren, it would have been &quot;probably futile&quot; (which seem to imply that the OoH could have survived the effort). It seems that Vampire has less interest to add &quot;insult on injury to the Order&quot; than Mage, as the factoid of having to fight other enemies isn't included in the OoH Tradition book, in the section where they comment how the Tremere massacred them, nearly killing them all.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spellfire22</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure how close this is to canon, but this is my interpretation: in 1197, the rest of the Order of Hermes discovers that most of the Tremere have been turned into vampires. The Tremere are expelled from the Order of Hermes, with the few non-vampiric magi of House Tremere joining other houses, primarily House Tytalus. A brief war follows, which ends in a stalemate, and the Hermetics and the Tremere basically agree to leave each other alone after that. This is the situation which exists at the start of the Second Massasa War detailed in Blood Treachery.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sounds right to me. Some books say it was more apocaliptic than that for the OoH, but I preffer the &quot;stalemate version&quot;, which it's also supported.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-13-2017, 03:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T12:43:23-07:00","id":1077857,"title":"","content":"I admit, I've always read the individual Tradition Books as being mostly IC propaganda. If there's a contradiction between the material, or something doesn't make sense to me, I usually just assume the Tradition Book is representing what the Masters are teaching their Acolytes, and may not be intended as actual fact.<br /> <br /> In this case, as prideful as the Hermetics are, I can imagine them trying to warn their students away from directly engaging with vampires when there is so much else going on. By exaggerating their losses in the First Massasa War, they discourage students from trying for a third, and get to celebrate an inspiring rise from the ashes story to boot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T16:31:28-07:00","id":1078018,"title":"","content":"I've touched upon it briefly in the thread <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1074903-having-a-hard-time-buying-into-the-tremere\">Having a Hard Time buying into the Tremere</a> but there are a few things I've found while looking into the history of the Tremere.<ul><li>In 1003 Tremere convinces House Flambeau and House Tytalus to launch a Wizard's March against the Druidic House Diedne with accusations of diabolism.<ul><li>1012 House Diedne is destroyed outside of rumors that some may have survived. Who knows how many Mages Flambeau and Tytalus, the two Houses best for the front line against the Tremere, lost in the war. As well the eventual revelation that the whole reasoning was false might convince more than a few Mages to sit out the next Wizard's March.<ul><li>How many mages were lost in the fighting especially since attrition is likely to be higher among newer mages. These same lost mages who will no longer be available as masters two centuries from now when the Massasa War begins.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> <li>The original Tremere founders performed their ritual in 1022 while the Order of Hermes did not find out about the issue and launch their Wizard's March starting the First Massasa War until 1202. In the meanwhile.<ul><li>During this time Tremere and others of his clan have access to the great Hermetic libraries which they are going through voraciously looking for knowledge and lore to adapt into Thaumaturgy.<ul><li>This has the added benefit of meaning that at the war's start the Tremere are relatively up to date on the magical research of the Order since they were specifically looking for powers and rituals to adapt.</li> </ul></li> <li>1121 Goratrix and Virstania create the first Gargoyles, providing proof that Thaumaturgy 5 must have been developed at that point.<ul><li>Not only are Gargoyles useful shock troops against the Tzimisce but their Fortitude means they have a defense against the likeliest effects the Order will make use of making them effective against the Order as well.</li> </ul></li> <li>1133 Tremere finds the resting place of and diablerizes Saulot in the Anatolian desert thus giving the Tremere a 3rd Generation Cainite. This also began their destruction of the Salubri clan which would have offered the opportunity for many Tremere to diablerize lower Generation Salubri giving the young clan a considerable boost in power.</li> <li>1197 Multiple statted Tremere in House of Tremere (who are weaker than Tremere, Goratrix, and Etrius) have achieved Thaumaturgy 6 which could mean other Tremere have other Disciplines at 6+ by that point especially the original founders. Considering his diablerie of Saulot 64 years prior its possible Tremere could have developed level 10 in Thaumaturgy and possibly other Disciplines (especially Auspex or Dominate) if not his mastery is likely between 8-10.</li> </ul></li> <li>The Tremere realized that at some point the Order would find out about what they did and were actively doing and would move against them. As such they had been preparing for that eventuality for nearly 200 years. The Massasa War itself would last for 80 years according to House of Tremere which would put the end year 1282.<ul><li>Having been a former Hermetic House they had a great deal of knowledge about exactly what was available and what could be brought to bear against them. As such they could work to counter them.<ul><li>Not all members of House Tremere had yet been converted so they still had some awakened members to aid them in the fight.</li> <li>The Thaumaturgical Path Perdo Magica was specifically designed to counter magical effects especially Hermetic ones.</li> <li>The Hermetic Order has much less information on vampires, especially since they are now going to war with the House that would have been most informed. So the Order is moving with little knowledge against a foe that has intimate knowledge and nearly two centuries of preparation.</li> <li>They had been part of a Wizard's March themselves and so had a solid grasp of exactly how the Order would go about another one even beyond their knowledge of normal Order functions.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> <li>The Blood Bond is effective against Mages and they may not realize what has occurred before it is too late. Dominate can also be effective especially against those who do not have mystical mental defenses.<ul><li>Suborn a few of the Mages and the Tremere can begin getting information on planned attacks and troop movements of the Order allowing them to move to ambush and counter them.</li> <li>The Tremere are already on a war footing with siege mentality because of the Tzimisce thus they already have agents watching for movements among mortal groups that could signal an offensive from their foes. So even without gaining access to Order plans they can still be forewarned of what's coming.</li> </ul></li> <li>As mentioned above Order losses are going to be felt far more acutely than Tremere ones. The Tremere can embrace more mortals to aid their cause whereas the Order has no control over awakenings and a newly made vampire is going to be more effective than a mundane mortal recruited by the Order.<ul><li>On the topic of embrace remember that it removes the magic the Mage once had. How terrifying of a prospect is that once the Order realizes that especially if they draw the obvious parallels to Gilgul. Some of the Masters who are supposed to be leading the conflict may shy away from the possibility not only of their death but the desecration of their Avatar.<ul><li>Additionally every Mage embrace is going to give the Tremere more information on what the Order's current moves are against them as well as information on any magical discoveries that are being disseminated among the soldiers.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> <li>Also the loss of reliability of magical effects that drove the Tremere to seek out alternative immortality is going to affect the Order members now fighting them.<ul><li>Imagine the blow to morale when something along these lines happen. A Gargoyle is bearing down on a Mage who goes to throw a fireball to incinerate the creature. He goes to cast the effect which had cast so many times before without issue and... It. Does. Not. Work. The Mages around him see this stone beast crash into the Mage and either start guzzling the Mage's blood or smashing his fleshly frame.<ul><li>As time goes on more and more of these stories of staple effects failing when the Mages needed them most are going to spread. Every time a Mage goes into battle the thought is going to be there in the back of their mind 'is this the battle where my powers fail'. What kind of drain on the willingness to fight will that be?</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> <li>Lastly as mentioned above the Tremere didn't really win and the Order didn't really lose the Massasa War. Both sides instead stepped away from the conflict to focus on more pressing issues: The Tremere facing the beginnings of the Anarch Revolt and the Order of Hermes facing the rise of the Order of Reason and the Ascension War. They realized continuing to throw more resources at the other was a lost cause.<ul><li>One thing to consider is that while vampires function more on an eternal scale much of the support structure of the Order is built around mortals. 80 years means that the bulk of the structure that was alive at the start of the war would now be dead from natural causes even without the conflict. In the same way those who awoke and survived the early nights of the war are now making use of effects to extend their lifespan or looking at a rapidly approaching death. They are going to be less willing to risk themselves.<ul><li>On the upper end of the scale the newer archmasters are going to find that the ongoing conflict no longer draws their attention as it once did since they are now concerned with a new echelon of powers and issues.</li> </ul></li> <li>The proponents of the war on the Order side are going to have a harder and harder time explaining their lack of gains or progress against the Tremere. Many more peace minded or less martially minded Mages are going to look at the mounting losses and hold them as proof of the monumental waste of the conflict. Not only are they continually suffering casualties their foe grows and thrives in comparison because of continually embracing new vampires.<ul><li>This argument is even more potent if the Order comes to understand how vampirism affects the Avatar because each Mage lost to Tremere embrace is an Avatar with Order memories that cannot reincarnate.</li> <li>You'll also have those who will argue that the Tremere weren't a problem until the Order launched their Wizard's March against them. Before that the Order members didn't have to worry about vampires or becoming them you didn't have constant casualties coming through Eastern Europe.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> </ul><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2008-cynic01","name":"Cynic01","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"437"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T18:14:03-07:00","id":1078059,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1077851#post1077851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The 100 Awakened population deserves no more atention that the normal given to those &quot;censes&quot;. Mages are always hit with the &quot;rare&quot; stick (to the point of saying that in modern days there are 1 per 1.000.000 people, which would put the OoH arround 333-400 members between all Houses) so I don't know if &quot;100&quot; it's 5% of what the Order had before the war, 20%, or 75%, especially if you count the House Tremere as part of the looses.<br /> Since you get to play with the OoH in DA: Mage, which despite the recent looses, it's still one of the most powerful mage groups arround 1230 (after the Massa War had cooled down) I would say that they weren't as crippled as the Tradition book seems to imply.<br /> <br /> I think it's worth to note that the War lasted until 1230 not because the OoH was dying, but because the Church started their Inquisiton. It seems that Hermetics weren't hiding their black robes and pointy hats at the moment, because they were hit *hard* by it. Supposedly Goroatrix wasn't above infiltrating the Inquisition to send them after mages, so that helped (this may have backfired after a time. Goroatrix was accused of calling attention to vampires because of his &quot;careless manipulations&quot;. Maybe the Church doesn't need a century to figure out they're being dupped by vampires, or maybe it was just that, for Etrius, Goroatrix it's the excuse of anything bad that happens under the moon).<br /> <br /> Any case, <i>as per Players Guide of the Low Clans, p. 62. </i>the Church (and the Messianic Voices?) was thought to be a&quot;good deal more immediately threatening to the Order than the Tremere&quot;. Perhaps because the Tremere didn't want to fight against their former brethren for sentimental reasons. I want to note that the book states that, while the Tremere didn't try to stamp out their former brethren, it would have been &quot;probably futile&quot; (which seem to imply that the OoH could have survived the effort). It seems that Vampire has less interest to add &quot;insult on injury to the Order&quot; than Mage, as the factoid of having to fight other enemies isn't included in the OoH Tradition book, in the section where they comment how the Tremere massacred them, nearly killing them all.</div> </div> </div> </div>No. Medieval OoH had more than a thousand members at the very least, both in MtA and Ars Magica. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11780-posthumanity","name":"Posthumanity","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T18:58:16-07:00","id":1078089,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Posthumanity</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1078059#post1078059\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No. Medieval OoH had more than a thousand members at the very least, both in MtA and Ars Magica.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hi. Could you tell me where to find this? It's killing me. I'm pretty sure I saw that info on numbers of mages during that time somewhere weeks ago and now can't seem to find it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T19:04:55-07:00","id":1078096,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1078089#post1078089\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hi. Could you tell me where to find this? It's killing me. I'm pretty sure I saw that info on numbers of mages during that time somewhere weeks ago and now can't seem to find it.</div> </div> </div> </div>1. Bonisagus: 81 mages (52 Bonisagi, 29 Trianomae);<br /> 2. Quaesitor: 98 (plus 41 non Guernitus Quaesitor);<br /> 3. Flambeau: 114 (19 in Proven\u00e7al Tribunal);<br /> 4. Tytalus: 96;<br /> 5. Verditius: 74;<br /> 6. Ex Micellanea: estimated 180 mages;<br /> 7. Mercere: 150 (12 &quot;awakened&quot;);<br /> 8. Tremere: 92 (41 in Transylvania);<br /> 9. Jerbition: 102 (26 in Tribunal of the Great Alps);<br /> 10. Bjomaer: 79;<br /> 11. Merinita: 68;<br /> 12 Criamon: 94;<br /> <br /> Approximately 1,230 magi in total in 12th century. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11780-posthumanity","name":"Posthumanity","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T19:48:20-07:00","id":1078123,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Posthumanity</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">No. Medieval OoH had more than a thousand members at the very least, both in MtA and Ars Magica.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, then it's a stupid number with even more reason. Better go with Kalinara's reasoning and say that the Tradition Book just wants to scare young mages so they don't go kill vampires. After all, the Order just had to deal with a plage of Ghouls among it's members. A way to avoid other it's to discourage hunting vampires altogether.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cynic01</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">How many mages were lost in the fighting especially since attrition is likely to be higher among newer mages. These same lost mages who will no longer be available as masters two centuries from now when the Massasa War begins.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think the death count was comparable to the Massa War because Diedne was an insular house with very few members (the most powerful of which escaped). Plus, it has never been treated with the apocaliptic vive the massa war has.<br /> <br /> As how of a valid point this is deppends on how useful do you think each mage it's. Probably they would have been better with Diedne and a few more Flambeau Masters than without them but I don't think of it as a game-changer (House Diedne probably would have done what it always did, criticize and do nothing).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The original Tremere founders performed their ritual in 1022 while the Order of Hermes did not find out about the issue and launch their Wizard's March starting the First Massasa War until 1202.<br /> In the meanwhile.During this time Tremere and others of his clan have access to the great Hermetic libraries which they are going through voraciously looking for knowledge and lore to adapt into Thaumaturgy<br /> .This has the added benefit of meaning that at the war's start the Tremere are relatively up to date on the magical research of the Order since they were specifically looking for powers and rituals to adap </div> </div> </div>Agreed. Albeit I don't buy how they were able to stay hidden for that long, they did. They had the mage lore, and that's no small thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Not only are Gargoyles useful shock troops against the Tzimisce but their Fortitude means they have a defense against the likeliest effects the Order will make use of making them effective against the Order as well. </div> </div> </div>Agreed, albeit Fortitude it's kind of a Meh. discipline. Fortitude isn't too intimidating agains the kind of damage Mages can manage.<br /> <br /> I don't get why Gargoyles were so damn effective against the Tzimisce, they don't seem THAT powerful to me, especialy when compared with the kind of monsters the Tzimisce could bring to the table, like the vozod.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 1133 Tremere finds the resting place of and diablerizes Saulot in the Anatolian desert thus giving the Tremere a 3rd Generation Cainite. This also began their destruction of the Salubri clan which would have offered the opportunity for many Tremere to diablerize lower Generation Salubri giving the young clan a considerable boost in power.1197 Multiple statted Tremere in House of Tremere (who are weaker than Tremere, Goratrix, and Etrius) have achieved Thaumaturgy 6 which could mean other Tremere have other Disciplines at 6+ by that point especially the original founders. Considering his diablerie of Saulot 64 years prior its possible Tremere could have developed level 10 in Thaumaturgy and possibly other Disciplines (especially Auspex or Dominate) if not his mastery is likely between 8-10 </div> </div> </div>They had a powerbase built for the time the war started, but this fact does not explain how they were able to build that power base with all the hostility they suffered before the war.<br /> <br /> It may be necessary to add that, as per the Players Guide to the Low Clans, Chantries ruled by vampiric Tremere were very weak against Tzimisce attacks. That's why Tremere was smart and decided to NOT turn his House into vampires (yet). Experienced mages fought (and died) against the Tzimisce while the vampires in their midst were able to develop vampiric power until they were ready to face any challenge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Tremere realized that at some point the Order would find out about what they did and were actively doing and would move against them. As such they had been preparing for that eventuality for nearly 200 years. The Massasa War itself would last for 80 years according to House of Tremere which would put the end year 1282.<ul><li>Having been a former Hermetic House they had a great deal of knowledge about exactly what was available and what could be brought to bear against them. As such they could work to counter them.<ul><li>Not all members of House Tremere had yet been converted so they still had some awakened members to aid them in the fight.</li> <li>The Thaumaturgical Path Perdo Magica was specifically designed to counter magical effects especially Hermetic ones.</li> <li>The Hermetic Order has much less information on vampires, especially since they are now going to war with the House that would have been most informed. So the Order is moving with little knowledge against a foe that has intimate knowledge and nearly two centuries of preparation.</li> <li>They had been part of a Wizard's March themselves and so had a solid grasp of exactly how the Order would go about another one even beyond their knowledge of normal Order functions.</li> </ul></li> </ul> </div> </div> </div>Very important points. I agree, but I doubt that moeTremere would fight for them agains the Order unless they've been Bonded (not even vampiric ex-mages trully *wanted* to fight agains the Order, such it's the loyalty Hermetics have). Tremere defectors where the ones that revealed the whole scheme to the Order after all. However, they probaby had some awakened *resources* in the form of Talismans, Trinkets, Wards, supernatural alies (you want to get dragons? Because that's how you get dragons).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Blood Bond is effective against Mages and they may not realize what has occurred before it is too late. Dominate can also be effective especially against those who do not have mystical mental defenses. </div> </div> </div>I agree that the Bond would have provided important, but mental and physiological manipulation it's nothing new to the Hermetics. And the Bond can be turned agains't the Tremere. Hermetics had the resources to device a spell to break the bond, which then could be ussed to free Tremere Ghouls, and get information on their masters, and even use them as moles (it's not like the sire knows). With this spell in hand, &quot;immunity to the bond&quot;, and &quot;create blood bond&quot; are just permuctations.<br /> <br /> Dominate it's not a gamechanger at all, as it's similar to what Hermetics have been doing for centuries with Mind (or the equivalent Pillar, Corona). I'm not saying that it isn't &quot;useful&quot;, just that isn't more worthwhile to mention than Lure of the Flames. Maybe less so, as Mages have really big Willpowers.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As mentioned above Order losses are going to be felt far more acutely than Tremere ones. The Tremere can embrace more mortals to aid their cause whereas the Order has no control over awakenings and a newly made vampire is going to be more effective than a mundane mortal recruited by the Order. </div> </div> </div>True, and it's a gamechanger for sure, but people puts more faith in this than they should. Recently embraced vampires aren't going to win a war against powerful supernatural foes, as Tremere himself discovered at the hands of the Tzimisce.<br /> <br /> It also should be mentioned that mages can enhance the equipment and bodies of their mortal followers, so it's not like acolites need to be cannon fooder when fighting against neonate vampires. In fact, this strategy it's often more effective (in a cost-efficiency way of thinking) than to blindly send young mages.<br /> <br /> While &quot;mass embrace&quot; doesn't really work in this setting, embracing it's still a gamechanger because, if you don't waste those vampires you just embraced, you can teach them to become master Thaumaturges, and that's something very effective.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On the topic of embrace remember that it removes the magic the Mage once had. How terrifying of a prospect is that once the Order realizes that especially if they draw the obvious parallels to Gilgul. Some of the Masters who are supposed to be leading the conflict may shy away from the possibility not only of their death but the desecration of their Avatar. </div> </div> </div>Masters were always shy of the conflict (in truth, they're more usefull back in their towers where they can prepare the next big ritual to help the Order). However, I don't think that the paralel to Gilgul would make mages cull with terror, on the contrary, it would enrage them even more. The whole Second Massa War was started with this argument (the vampires not only kill our people, they destroy their souls!)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Additionally every Mage embrace is going to give the Tremere more information on what the Order's current moves are against them as well as information on any magical discoveries that are being disseminated among the soldiers. </div> </div> </div>Maybe not &quot;every&quot; mage, remember that people you embraced by force in war might hate you despite the 1rst level bond, you can't really trust anything they said until they've got fully Bonded or Dominated (at wich point they're not that different from any captive).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also the loss of reliability of magical effects that drove the Tremere to seek out alternative immortality is going to affect the Order members now fighting them.<br /> Imagine the blow to morale when something along these lines happen. A Gargoyle is bearing down on a Mage who goes to throw a fireball to incinerate the creature. He goes to cast the effect which had cast so many times before without issue and... It. Does. Not. Work. The Mages around him see this stone beast crash into the Mage and either start guzzling the Mage's blood or smashing his fleshly frame.<br /> As time goes on more and more of these stories of staple effects failing when the Mages needed them most are going to spread. Every time a Mage goes into battle the thought is going to be there in the back of their mind 'is this the battle where my powers fail'. What kind of drain on the willingness to fight will that be? </div> </div> </div>Now I have to disagree entirely with your point. You misunderstand what &quot;the loss of reliability of magical effects&quot; means.<br /> <br /> Powerful effects, like the longevity potion started to become more &quot;difficult&quot; and cost more Vis, but small magic(k)s like a fireball weren't less reliable, it's just that Botches started to become more severe (they produce Backlash).<br /> If a minor thing like a wound for Botching to cast a Fireball it's going to traumatize Mages, then what would happen with the Tremere, that loose a whole Permanent Willpower point each time thei Botch? .<br /> Dark Ages magely fireballs aren't less reliable than vampiric ones. Mighty permanent rituals?, yes, they were less reliable.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Lastly as mentioned above the Tremere didn't really win and the Order didn't really lose the Massasa War. Both sides instead stepped away from the conflict to focus on more pressing issues: The Tremere facing the beginnings of the Anarch Revolt and the Order of Hermes facing the rise of the Order of Reason and the Ascension War. They realized continuing to throw more resources at the other was a lost cause.<br /> <br /> One thing to consider is that while vampires function more on an eternal scale much of the support structure of the Order is built around mortals. 80 years means that the bulk of the structure that was alive at the start of the war would now be dead from natural causes even without the conflict.<br /> <br /> In the same way those who awoke and survived the early nights of the war are now making use of effects to extend their lifespan or looking at a rapidly approaching death. They are going to be less willing to risk themselves.<br /> On the upper end of the scale the newer archmasters are going to find that the ongoing conflict no longer draws their attention as it once did since they are now concerned with a new echelon of powers and issues </div> </div> </div>Agreed with all of that. Even before the rise of the OoR, in the Dark Ages, the OoH had other enemies, (as per the book I've been mentioning lately), and the battle had it's &quot;cold&quot; moments after 1230, where the Hermetics and the Tremere wouldn't attack each other unless they casualy met in the same place. According to the book, this actualy caused problems with Tremere's recuiting methods. The reason they started to embrace outside the occult and academic circles was in part because these are the same places where Hermetic recuit their members, and thus embracing there could hotten the conflict.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-13-2017, 10:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T19:57:23-07:00","id":1078128,"title":"","content":"Here's a Thread where the logistics of a war between Hermetics and Tremere were discussed (and both massa wars were mentioned, of course). It may be interesting for those who like this topic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/379957-how-anyone-tried-out-a-hermetic-tremere-war\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/379957-how-anyone-tried-out-a-hermetic-tremere-war\"> how anyone tried out a hermetic-tremere war? - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/379957-how-anyone-tried-out-a-hermetic-tremere-war\"> http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/379957-how-anyone-tried-out-a-hermetic-tremere-war </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> was it balanced? as i understand thaumaturgy essentially it's ,main difference from magick is that all it's good stuff come from long rituals many of whom have rather bizarre components. they also seem lesscapable at attacking from afar and dealing direct damage. assuming some reasonable rulings( giving </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-13T22:20:47-07:00","id":1078182,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1078123#post1078123\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think the death count was comparable to the Massa War because Diedne was an insular house with very few members (the most powerful of which escaped). Plus, it has never been treated with the apocaliptic vive the massa war has.<br /> As how of a valid point this is deppends on how useful do you think each mage it's. Probably they would have been better with Diedne and a few more Flambeau Masters than without them but I don't think of it as a game-changer (House Diedne probably would have done what it always did, criticize and do nothing).</div> </div> </div> </div>Perhaps but each Mage is valuable. More mentioning it because it gives the Tremere intricate knowledge of exactly the way a Wizard's March is going to be executed and because it may make some Mages wary of doing the same thing again and ending up weaker for it again.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Agreed, albeit Fortitude it's kind of a Meh. discipline. Fortitude isn't too intimidating agains the kind of damage Mages can manage.<br /> I don't get why Gargoyles were so damn effective against the Tzimisce, they don't seem THAT powerful to me, especialy when compared with the kind of monsters the Tzimisce could bring to the table, like the vozod. </div> </div> </div>Fortitude may not be as intimidating it does give them a defense where many other clans would lack one. Vohzd are cost prohibitive and difficult to field in large numbers since it requires a level 6 Koldunic Sorcery ritual or an Animalism 6 Vicissitude 6 combination power. As for why they were so effective against the Tzimisce I have a few thoughts on that.<ul><li>Potence: While great for dealing damage on its own, remember Tremere have the Level 2 Ritual Flaming Weapon. Give them a weapon like a Flaming battle axe, halberd, or warhammer and you have a Gargoyle throwing Strength + Potence (likely turned into auto successes) + Bonus successes + 4 Aggravated damage, against a clan that does not have Fortitude as a clan Discipline (example Strength 4, Dexterity 3, Melee 3, Potence 1 Gargoyle: Not counting specialty successes you have anywhere from 0-6 bonus dice on attack which translates to 8-14 dice of Aggravated damage + 1 auto success. This can easily one shot a Tzimisce since they do not have Fortitude as a clan Discipline). Also note that barring an effect Mages cannot soak Aggravated damage either.</li> <li>Flight: The advantage being able to get out of and engage beyond the reach of your opponents should not be ignored. Tzimisce cannot directly engage with an airborne Gargoyle outside of archery or thrown weapons until they reach Vicissitude 6 Chiropteran Marauder whereas a Gargoyle could fly soon after embrace. Imagine this divebombing Gargoyle armed with the Flaming Weapon like above or a spear Enchanted with Shaft of Belated Quiescence.</li> <li>Gargoyle Rituals (DAV20): These can be placed as permanent effects on Gargoyles which is huge. Here are the more combat focused ones.<ul><li>Donning the Mask of Shadows: How difficult would it be to spot a hazy shape against a dark cloudy sky? I imagine the surprise of a Gargoyle suddenly appearing before them probably led to the demise of a few Tzimisce.</li> <li>Firewalker: Immunity to Rotschreck and bonus 5 extra dice to soak fire over their Fortitude. This makes it so Tremere can lob Lure of Flames/Creo Ignem/Creatio Ignis fires into their foes and not worry their shock troops will suffer for it. Additionally it makes the go to fire effects from the Order less effective as well.</li> <li>Heart of Stone: Getting twice stamina dice as a bonus soak vs staking is immensely likely to prevent it (especially since Gargoyles are likely to have high Stamina ratings). The protection against supernatural emotional manipulation (such as Presence) is not shabby either.</li> <li>Stone Slumber: The Gargoyle does not have to fear the rays of the sun becoming magical unbreakable stone making it easy to have a force ready for when night falls. Also could be an interesting combat trick: start the fight very near dawn, grapple your foe and once the sun rises they are trapped by an unbreakable stone grip and the sun burns them to ash.</li> </ul></li> <li>Gargoyle Powers (Guide to the Low Clans): While not all are as effective in combat a few stand out.<ul><li>Celerity: Multiple attacks are good especially with Flaming Weapons.</li> <li>Fire Breath: Three dice of aggravated damage that benefits from bonus successes on the attack roll carrying over to damage is amazing. Gargoyles are probably going to have decent Dexterity and high Athletics ratings so they are likely to have a high dice pool vs difficulty 6. Tzimisce do not have Fortitude as a clan Discipline so that 3+attack successes dice of damage can easily get within range of one-shotting a vampire (lets say an average Gargoyle likely having a Dexterity of 3-4 and an Athletics of 3-5 is going to be throwing a pool of 6-9 dice of which bonus successes carry over with an additional 3 dice: this average Gargoyle can thus be throwing 3-11 dice of Aggravated damage which many Tzimisce cannot soak). Also remember without an effect Mages cannot soak Aggravated damage either.</li> <li>Regeneration: Can heal with blood regardless of exertion, and healing even a point or two of Aggravated damage in combat can be the difference in outlasting your foe.</li> </ul></li> </ul><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hermetics had the resources to device a spell to break the bond, which then could be ussed to free Tremere Ghouls, and get information on their masters, and even use them as moles (it's not like the sire knows). With this spell in hand, &quot;immunity to the bond&quot;, and &quot;create blood bond&quot; are just permuctations.<br /> <br /> Maybe not &quot;every&quot; mage, remember that people you embraced by force in war might hate you despite the 1rst level bond, you can't really trust anything they said until they've got fully Bonded or Dominated (at wich point they're not that different from any captive). </div> </div> </div>Only takes 3 nights to completely blood bond someone at which point you have a slavishly loyal servant of the Tremere. As for such a power the Order has to have the opportunity to use it (so bonding a captured Mage and they using him in battle with the command to counterspell the enemies effects could be very valuable). Just as the Order can develop undo/immunity to bond so can the Tremere create an effect to counter that. In the end you end up with some weird cold war style situations where agents end up swapping sides further leading to the whole is it worth throwing more resources at this sentiment.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now I have to disagree entirely with your point. You misunderstand what &quot;the loss of reliability of magical effects&quot; means.<br /> Powerful effects, like the longevity potion started to become more &quot;difficult&quot; and cost more Vis, but small magic(k)s like a fireball weren't less reliable, it's just that Botches started to become more severe (they produce Backlash).<br /> If a minor thing like a wound for Botching to cast a Fireball it's going to traumatize Mages, then what would happen with the Tremere, that loose a whole Permanent Willpower point each time thei Botch? .<br /> Dark Ages magely fireballs aren't less reliable than vampiric ones. Mighty permanent rituals?, yes, they were less reliable. </div> </div> </div>In V20 a botch does not guarantee a loss of a Willpower dot that is simply one of the possible backlashes. DAV20 meanwhile gives a specific backlash for each lesser Path (Creatio Ignis [Lure of Flames] for example does a level of Aggravated damage to the caster on a botch). Part of my though process was reading heavily into the way the Tremere panicked at the thought.<br /> <br /> I'm not as familiar with the Dark Ages Pillars as I am with the Spheres (I play M20 regularly) so I don't know if the following is correct. Its much easier for a Tremere to have a Willpower of 7+ to use a Thaumaturgical effect than a Mage to have a high Arete meaning the Mage is more likely to botch than the Tremere because of the lower die pool. Basically Im thinking the average Tremere is using a die pool of 6-9 for his effect whereas the Mage is likely around 2-4 the Mage thus more likely to not have the success to prevent the one from botching. Additionally each time that Mage accrues Paradox it increases the chance of a backlash since they are likely throwing Vulgar fireball type effects (since fire works well against vampires).<br /> <br /> Another thing to mention more related to their survival against the Tzimisce are political issues essentially causing the Tzimisce to never fully focus their efforts on the Tremere or not have access to them.<ul><li>The Tzimisce were incredibly fractious and warred with each other as often as the Tremere if not more so. Some examples<ul><li>Voivode of Voivode Vladimir Rustovitch's own broodmates sold him out to the Shadow Lords who came in and laid waste to his estate and slew his men while Rustovitch was off at war.</li> <li>Dracon destroyed Triglav because he thought the latter had done something to Gesu because he fell into torpor upon embrace since the Transylvanian Tzimisce had voiced against it.</li> <li>Noriz the former Voivode of Voivodes continually launched Trials by War against various Tzimisce lords including the Methuselah Yorak and is described as continually marching his armies across Moldavia.</li> <li>The Anarch Revolt and the diablerie of the Tzimisce Antediluvian grew out of the issues the younger Tzimisce had with their elders.</li> </ul></li> <li>The Ventrue were attempting to push into Tzimisce lands through the Holy Roman Empire via Hungary. These actions are going to distract the Tzimisce and pull away many vital resources and troops to stop their activities.<ul><li>The Tremere also made many entreaties to the Ventrue and offered their services which the Ventrue would make use of. In the beginning the Tremere would make useful catspaws in their intrigues against the Tzimisce. Later this patronage would eventually develop into a fairly stable alliance, there is a reason the Tremere were part of the Camarilla despite having a history filled with diablerie.</li> <li>The Ventrue's position in the Holy Roman Empire essentially cut off the Tzimisce from seeking some of the Chantries of the Tremere.<ul><li>Vienna is well within the HRE's protection and thus the Ventrue who are not going to let the Tzimisce violate that. This is one of the big reasons beyond Etrius's preference that this became the new head Chantry of the clan.</li> <li>Paris has the entirety of Europe as a buffer to the Tzimisce strongholds in the Carpathians.</li> <li>The British Tremere are even further removed by land and sea from the Carpathians.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> </ul><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2008,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2008-cynic01","user":"Cynic01","date":". 04-14-2017, 01:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2008-cynic01","name":"Cynic01","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"437"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-14T06:53:15-07:00","id":1078312,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Five Eyes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1077807#post1077807\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You gotta figure that even the most well-attended Wizard March only mobilizes like 20% of the Order of Hermes, really. I still think the Tremere's survival is pretty miraculous given how many people they're supposed to have pissed off, but I'd say that antagonizing the Tzimisce in the seat of their power is far more threatening that torquing off the (relatively widely-distributed and non-cooperative) Order of Hermes. The Order may have some people in it that can annihilate whole chantries with meteors from space, but it's composed of the sort of people that wouldn't bother to do so just out of loyalty to the organization. The latter medieval period is supposed to be a decadent era for the Hermetics - they're magically powerful, sure, but they're also at one of their most dysfunctional points as a fellowship.<br /> <br /> (I think the &quot;less than 100&quot; number in the Revised OoH book should probably just be ignored, in any case: It's always a bad idea for WoD stuff to list numbers.)</div> </div> </div> </div>And I'd like to point out that the reclusive Tzimisce were uncooperative with each other at best, they worked together on a base of the same &quot;brood-ties&quot;, which means that initially the Tremere did had to work their way only against some local Tzimisce lords, and things probably didn't escalated until, later, they pissed off some low-generation high-standing Voivode.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>====================================<br /> &quot;Worst English-language User&quot; 2011-2015, 2017 running for the 2018<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ed6RV6Z.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></div></div> </div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/515-ryoga84","name":"Ryoga84","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar515_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-14T07:03:53-07:00","id":1078320,"title":"","content":"Aside from mechanical comparisons - one thing the Tremere did very well is to create enemies for their enemies. The Tzimisce were pissed - so they allied with the Germanic Ventrue that were trying to expand into Tzimisce lands. Now a Tzimisce lord knows that if he spends too much power against the Tremere chantry next door, he might lose his land altogether - and he might play it safe.<br /> <br /> Also, remember that the reason Mistridge fell to the Craftmasons is that Grimgroth, a Tremere mage, lowered its defenses at a critical time<br /> <br /> Where the Tytalans believe in personal strength to overcome obstacles, the Tremere believe in weakening enemies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-14T15:14:13-07:00","id":1078570,"title":"","content":"For the same reason that the Order of Hermes lost to the Order of Reason.<br /> <br /> They're obsessively hidebound, and arrogant to the point of disregarding potential threats. They don't adapt to their enemies until forced to, and even then not always. Tremere, for all his dumbassery, was not really the most 'hermetic' of hermetics. He had no problems with thinking outside the box and using tools outside the traditional.<br /> <br /> Combine that with the fact that Tremere as a house was excessively focused on politics, intrigue, and dueling, and you get a recipe for disaster.<br /> <br /> The hilarious thing is I started into this going &quot;I don't really know. Mages would beat twelve colors of hell out of any upstart vampires that crossed them.&quot; And then I realized that we were talking about the Order of Hermes. And as stylish as they are, they're also complete and utter morons about their magic, and none of the tactics I could think of to really counter an intelligently played vampire rebellion would actually fly with the Hermes ethos.<br /> <br /> For example, the point about mortals not being as good as newly awakened vampires. To that I say bullshit. Cook up some alchemical concoction with life/time 3 to give them super reflexes and 5's in all their physical stats. Arm them with +1 swords of vampire rending or whatever. Boots with wings. The works.<br /> <br /> Then I remembered &quot;This is the Order of Hermes. They HATE giving the proles the time of day. Let alone giving them ARTIFACTS WITH FRAGMENTS OF THEIR WILL.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11744,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","user":"Enginseer-42","date":". 04-14-2017, 06:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-14T15:22:03-07:00","id":1078577,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cynic01</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not as familiar with the Dark Ages Pillars as I am with the Spheres (I play M20 regularly) so I don't know if the following is correct. Its much easier for a Tremere to have a Willpower of 7+ to use a Thaumaturgical effect than a Mage to have a high Arete meaning the Mage is more likely to botch than the Tremere because of the lower die pool. Basically Im thinking the average Tremere is using a die pool of 6-9 for his effect whereas the Mage is likely around 2-4 the Mage thus more likely to not have the success to prevent the one from botching. Additionally each time that Mage accrues Paradox it increases the chance of a backlash since they are likely throwing Vulgar fireball type effects (since fire works well against vampires).</div> </div> </div> </div>Pillars use Foundation (Arete) + Pillar (Sphere level), so dicepools of 7 aren't that rare (and you can use Quintessence, which was a bit more ubiquitous back then). In the end, it's tangential, you COULD play the 1\u00b0 Massa War with Arete and Spheres.<br /> However, Paradox was nonexistant (in a Botch, you suffer backlash, a bit like what you depict for the Tremere, but more complicated, you don't accrue it the same way as Paradox). The Death of Magick was more a thing of ancient mages dying and mythical beasts and places disappearing tan Paradox, also, backlashes started to became more severe (but until the Renaissance nobody noticed). The Order woed the loss of ancient and great minds, and feared that, if the trend continued, all magick, even minor spells, would be lost.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for why (the Gargoyles) were so effective against the Tzimisce I have a few thoughts on that.<br /> ... </div> </div> </div>Interesting points. I can see how air combat can be a game-changer in medieval europe. Also, I see that, much like the mages they are, Tremere may be better empowering warriors to fight for them than going themselves, and thus having a bit more of muscle could have been a huge advantage (at least at that time).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haldir2012</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Aside from mechanical comparisons - one thing the Tremere did very well is to create enemies for their enemies. The Tzimisce were pissed - so they allied with the Germanic Ventrue that were trying to expand into Tzimisce lands...<br /> <br /> Where the Tytalans believe in personal strength to overcome obstacles, the Tremere believe in weakening enemies.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not necessarily so. I have to break a lance in favor of the Tytalans, they have a lot of (justified) bad press and have made a LOT of bad choices in their history, but as per Blood Treachery, they were the ones that dared to make aliances with the Tzimisce (some of which survived to the 2\u00b0 Massa War). To me, they earned the award for &quot;the House that actually tried to do<i> something</i> for the War (appart from throwing fireballs)&quot;.<br /> <br /> EDIT:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Enginseer-42</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">For example, the point about mortals not being as good as newly awakened vampires. To that I say bullshit. Cook up some alchemical concoction with life/time 3 to give them super reflexes and 5's in all their physical stats. Arm them with +1 swords of vampire rending or whatever. Boots with wings. The works.<br /> <br /> Then I remembered &quot;This is the Order of Hermes. They HATE giving the proles the time of day. Let alone giving them ARTIFACTS WITH FRAGMENTS OF THEIR WILL.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, but would you say that altering the proles with magick (instead of giving the proles magick items), so they get to be fearless (and expendable) combat monsters would be against they'r credo too? . I can see the Hermetics altering body and mind of the proles to make them more resistant and murdery.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-14-2017, 08:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-15T13:09:10-07:00","id":1079017,"title":"","content":"I think part of it may be that the mechanics don't do justice to how much of an advantage undeath would be in combat. By mechanics, a human mage shooting fireballs can easily one-shot a vampire, whereas a vampire shooting fireballs is only going to do three aggs to a mage, and would need three turns of fireballing to kill the mage. Going by story, it's a fight between humans who can't fight with most of their skin burned off, and undead horrors who can fight with most of their skin burned off, and then some.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-15T14:18:53-07:00","id":1079042,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CajunKhan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I think part of it may be that the mechanics don't do justice to how much of an advantage undeath would be in combat. By mechanics, a human mage shooting fireballs can easily one-shot a vampire, whereas a vampire shooting fireballs is only going to do three aggs to a mage, and would need three turns of fireballing to kill the mage. Going by story, it's a fight between humans who can't fight with most of their skin burned off, and undead horrors who can fight with most of their skin burned off, and then some.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fire it's supposed to be a Heavenly Bane to vampires...they shouldn't be able to &quot;fight with most of their skin burned off&quot; like you say, they should be running in Rottserch.<br /> <br /> Besides, mechanically, 7-8 dice of aggravated aren't &quot;skin burned off&quot;, they are &quot;a body being completely obliterated and turned to ash&quot;, be you a vampire or a mage.<br /> <br /> So, I don't agree with your wording, but what you're saying it's that &quot;Lure of the Flames&quot; doesn't traduce well to &quot;Flambeau Fireball&quot;. I agree with that. They should be more or less the same, but they aren't.<br /> <br /> Well, welcome to crossover.<br /> <br /> <i>Besides, doesn't Lure of the Flames do damage over time?, I mean, I still agree in that systems should be more compatible, but it's not like Lure wouldn't be effective, it just that it ain't going to be an &quot;instant&quot; kill, it will take a few turns but you don't need to cast it again.</i> <hr />[Rant]<br /> I also have complaints about how &quot;realistically represented&quot; this kind of stuff it's:<br /> <br /> Like, say, Tremere being able to pull whatever ritual they need of their ass while they're supposed to be unchanging undead wielding static magic. <i>Going by the story</i>, it should be living, dynamic, humans fighting statical undead lost to time that can't invent new tricks easily. Have you pondered on the adventage that creative people who can rewrite reality and reinvent their magick &quot;should represent in combat&quot;?<br /> <br /> It's funny how the superiror supernatural R&amp;D (so to speak) of mages it's never represented in these kind of long lasting conflicts (or anywhere, really), instead making mages go blasting vs the undead monsters in uncreative ways. <b>At night</b>. <i>By themselves</i>. <u>Without knowledge</u> about their foes. As if there wasn't a bajillon better strategies to employ agains't &quot;undead monsters&quot;<br /> Oh, but I forgot that mages have Hubris! (aka, excuse to be as dumb as the writer needs).<br /> <br /> With all their Hubris it's a wonder that the OoH are &quot;still&quot; the most powerful Tradition, one has to wonder how that did happened when the OoH can't make a strategic decision if their lives are on the line.<br /> <br /> Fortunately these 'ills' rarely happen to players.<br /> [/Rant]","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-15-2017, 05:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-15T21:40:40-07:00","id":1079225,"title":"","content":"To be fair, that's like 90% of what makes the Technocratic Union themselves. Using magick in an intelligent manner.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-16T07:52:56-07:00","id":1079363,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11744\" data-vbnamecard=\"11744\">Enginseer-42</a> :<br /> EXACTLY: And <b>they're the representation of Hubris in Mage!.</b><br /> The Hubris of believing themselves above humanity (what they despectively call &quot;the masses&quot;), they know &quot;what's good for you&quot;, their control of the world it's the only reasonable outcome!. That's Hubris! , acting like you own the world <i>because you do</i>, disregarding the consequences or the fuck-ups as minnor things that can be soaked by your power or enlightement.<br /> <br /> Going to a fight without using magick in an intelligent manner it's not Hubris. What the Technocracy does (believing so much in their own Hype as to trully think that they can justify any evil, because their actions will end in the greater good, inevitably) it's Hubris. Hubris brings forth the immense armies of walking Devices, the Starships, the magical weapons, to fight against the entire universe, disregarding the cost to the spirit, or the planet, or the Technocracy itself (that ends very corrupted). Believing you're beyond any oposition, that no one has the right of questioning your Paradigm, it's Hubris.<br /> <br /> Hubris can explain why Tremere turned into vampire, or why he tried to take on Saulot. Hubris shouldn't be an excuse to use dumb strategies, though. It's not like Tremere didn't do his research, it's not like he didn't had a good strategy, the thing it's that he dared to defy a god (in both instances), knowingly. That's Hubris.<br /> <br /> For a group that doesn't use magick inteligently, one has to wonder how the OoH has so much success among the Trads. Are the others just worse using magick?. No, the only explanation I can see it's that Hermetics do inteligent stuff out of camera.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-16-2017, 10:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-16T12:22:45-07:00","id":1079463,"title":"","content":"One thing that has yet to be pointed out in this thread, is which individual exactly was responsible for the fall of Mistridge? Suffice it to say from that, the Tremere didn't so much win the war as much as hold out until the Order of Hermes had much, <i>much</i> bigger fish to fry.<br /> <br /> Now, I'm not saying the Tremere inadvertently kicked off the Ascension War as a relief valve to prevent their destruction at the hands of a Wizards' March they'd long seen coming as soon as their betrayal inevitably came to light, but if it quacks, loves water, and has a corkscrew penis...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-16T14:33:11-07:00","id":1079517,"title":"","content":"I think Tremere himself is also an interesting factor. At least per House Tremere, the man spent a LOT of time between his own Embrace and diablerizing Saulot going from Chantry to Chantry collecting magical knowledge to convert to Thaumaturgy, and quelling and misdirecting doubts. He is supposed to be someone charismatic enough to have almost ended up commanding the entire Order at one point, and the supplement suggests that he may have engaged in some carefully chosen blood bonding or even Embraces along the way.<br /> <br /> One advantage that the Tremeres have, I think, is that pyramid power structure. I mean, there might be a lot of inter-level fighting (like Etrius and Goratrix), but as long as there is one central goal, the House (at the time anyway) was able to unify in a way that the other Hermetic Houses really couldn't. Arrogance and power struggles are pretty universal to all Hermetic Houses, but the Tremere apparently had someone whose job it was to specifically murder wayward apprentices, neonates, and subverted mortal mages in Ceoris, who endangered their secret before they were ready to act.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-17T00:30:29-07:00","id":1079707,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1079363#post1079363\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">EXACTLY: The Hubris of believing themselves above humanity (what they despectively call &quot;the masses&quot;)</div> </div> </div> </div>Still, the term is in fact infinitely more respective than &quot;the kine&quot;, &quot;the cattle&quot;, &quot;the bloodbags&quot; and so on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11780,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11780-posthumanity","user":"Posthumanity","date":". 04-17-2017, 03:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11780-posthumanity","name":"Posthumanity","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-17T00:40:24-07:00","id":1079710,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Posthumanity</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1079707#post1079707\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That is one of myriad reasons of why My hate burns so intense against vile hemophage parasites who regard themselves as living gods/rulers of humanity/leech-overlords/center of all multiverses, and to the lesser extant, to skinchangers.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok.......it's just a game of pretend people....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-17T00:46:13-07:00","id":1079713,"title":"","content":"Indeed. Sorry for offensive words. I deleted the comment.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11780-posthumanity","name":"Posthumanity","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-17T07:25:30-07:00","id":1079782,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1079463#post1079463\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing that has yet to be pointed out in this thread, is which individual exactly was responsible for the fall of Mistridge? Suffice it to say from that, the Tremere didn't so much win the war as much as hold out until the Order of Hermes had much, <i>much</i> bigger fish to fry.<br /> <br /> Now, I'm not saying the Tremere inadvertently kicked off the Ascension War as a relief valve to prevent their destruction at the hands of a Wizards' March they'd long seen coming as soon as their betrayal inevitably came to light, but if it quacks, loves water, and has a corkscrew penis...</div> </div> </div> </div>While I doubt that Goroatrix thought that the small band of rogue Hermetics that attacked Mistridge would become the strongest magical (if not supernatural) force in the world, I think it was very much in his impulsive character to aid any force that would oppose the OoH (just as he did with the Inquisition, but with less of a backlash)<br /> <br /> But see, this it's why I criticize so much the act of the OoH in the War, and say it's unrealistic:<br /> Tremeres did A LOT of things to win the war. They leaved no stone unturned, no potential aliance wasted, no &quot;enemy of my enemy&quot; unprovoqued and on top of that developped Paths and Rituals for the war. <br /> What did the Hermetic do in the century that the war lasted?. Well, there's two or so Rotes (from the people who can develop new rotes &quot;at fly&quot;). Tytalan did a tenuous aliance with a few elders, and while this wasn't mentioned as a true advantage, at least they tried to do something. And that's it.<br /> <br /> What mages could/should have done to win the war it's not studied, instead, we have to conclude that <b>A.</b> OoH didn't really participated (just a 20% or so fought), <b>B.</b> didn't resaearched their enemies, <b>C.</b> fought against armies of undead by themselves, <b>E.</b> were best in an armamentisitic scalement that they couldn't have been bested at by the rules,<b> F.</b> were surrounded by enemies that were much more effective than the Tzimisce without noticying that threat until it was too late ( It's amazing how-in spite of Sphere/Pillars representing a profound supernatural understanding of the concepts they represent, represented with &quot;Auspex like&quot; powers + the power to reduce the difficulty of related Skills-Mages never seem to have an advantage at gathering information/knowledge. They never seem to be more capable than humans, albeit they could be by the rules). This it's not the OoH being bested by the strategy or the power of their enemies, this is the OoH not doing anything against that strategy, not using their power inteligently (because, reasons).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-17T14:36:50-07:00","id":1080005,"title":"","content":"There really isn't much material on what the Order of Hermes did during that war but I feel that criticizing this for being unrealistic is like criticizing data you don't actually have.<br /> <br /> While the real reason for this is that writers weren't really allocated to writing anything truly in depth for these things, I'll have to say that the lack of material on what the Order of Hermes did doesn't have to mean that they didn't do much and that we don't &quot;have to conclude&quot; certain things. Maybe they did a lot of stuff that just wasn't recorded.<br /> <br /> For example: Perhaps the Order of Hermes did terrible things to try and win the war that backfired spectacularly and that they would rather not record. For example, what if they killed innocents in mass numbers to remove the possibility of these being turned into vampires and ended up with a rebellion wherein members of their own ranks began to willingly join the Tremere to take revenge on the &quot;Evil Wizards&quot;; Or, what if the mages tried to enchant the blood of the innocents to make them poisonous to vampires and instead ended up killing thousands of innocents... with the same results as above?; What if they tried to perform a great anti-vampire ritual that was disrupted by one Tremere noob using countermagick and the backlash opened up a gate to hell and killed enough elders that the Order wanted to bury that data?<br /> <br /> I mean, even in history, just because the tactics of one army are not recorded and &quot;lost to time&quot; doesn't mean we should assume that they didn't try anything, simply that what they tried did not work the way they wanted. I'd say, the Order of Hermes did stuff and did a bad enough job or were so bested that they just don't want to talk about it.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-17T14:52:32-07:00","id":1080021,"title":"","content":"With regards to the other points, let's put the war in a microcosm.<br /> Say for example, you are 10 mages who get together now and then. Each of you has a disciple mage and enough servants to support you so that you can perform your studies without needing to work. You note that one of your number has only been contacting you by mail for the past 100 years and think little of it until someone else brings up the possibility that that guy might be corrupted or something. The night after you bring this possibility to the leaders of your group, the group is beset by say 400 gargoyles supported by 100 vampires with countermagic as the first wave of the war. In the mean time the 18 mages have maybe only doubled in number to 36 or something. How is the larger scale war much different from this? Are these numbers off? Are there any problems with this scenario?<br /> <br /> As for rules, I'd say Vampire Dark Ages Vs. Mage Dark Ages would be the best fit for this sort of thing,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12480,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","user":"Spacecat","date":". 04-17-2017, 06:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-17T15:42:49-07:00","id":1080049,"title":"","content":"Jumping onto Spacecat's post above: I think a big issue is simply that the vast majority of material about the Massasa War is from the Tremere side. And that makes some sense. This is the Tremere origin story after all, and the Vampire: Dark Ages published a fairly substantial amount of books before going to Dark Ages Vampire and finally including Mage in that line up. Everything from the Hermetic perspective is written centuries after the fact. (Even Sorcerer's Crusade is almost 200 years later.)<br /> <br /> Sadly, there is no &quot;House Tremere&quot; type contemporaneous supplement that can illustrate the movements of the Hermetic Houses. Tosia's entry at least indicates that there is some suspicion/movement on that side. But that's a small hint compared to the focus on Tremere's schemes.<br /> <br /> I can buy that the Hermetics are willing to downplay their own schemes and stratagems in their accounts to their modern day fellows though. Even granted that the Tremere are probably the worst of the Order, we've seen enough of their pre-Embrace practices to realize that these are NOT nice people. I suspect most modern Hermetics would be disgusted at the rite to consecrate Ceoris and the callousness toward human life that we see among the mortal mages wouldn't sit well either.<br /> <br /> And well, with the Ascension War, the older Hermetics could have decided that it's best to downplay anything that might bring to mind some of the more sympathetic reasons that the Craftmasons may have had to turn against the Order of Hermes.<br /> <br /> Sadly, until or unless we end up seeing some Mage-side supplements for this time period, storytellers are stuck deciding the Hermetics' side of the war. That said, they did make it to a stalemate, so that would at least imply they put up a fight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-18T10:32:48-07:00","id":1080415,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">There really isn't much material on what the Order of Hermes did during that war but I feel that criticizing this for being unrealistic is like criticizing data you don't actually have.</div> </div> </div> </div>You didn't got my argument: We're discussing if it's realistic for the Tremere to have won the war. Thus, what the OoH could and should have done to win (and why they didn't do it) it's as much a part of the question as what the Tremre did or could have done. It's important to understand the advantages that the Tremere had over them. In the end, the conclusions of why they didn't won, were along the lines of the points I highlighted.<br /> <i>They're not my conclusion of what the OoH did, </i>I didn't say that 20% of the Order was involved, nor that countering blood bond and Dominate would cause a scalement where Tremere counter the developments of the OoH with rituals, nor that the battles would take the form of undead that should fight with their skin buring vs. humans that can't do the same, nor that they didn't knew about vampires, nor that they have to retreat because the Tremere set them against many enemies (well, ok, I did mentioned the Church).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> While the real reason for this is that writers weren't really allocated to writing anything truly in depth for these things, I'll have to say that the lack of material on what the Order of Hermes did doesn't have to mean that they didn't do much and that we don't &quot;have to conclude&quot; certain things. Maybe they did a lot of stuff that just wasn't recorded.<br /> <br /> For example: Perhaps the Order of Hermes did terrible things to try and win the war that backfired spectacularly and that they would rather not record. For example, what if they killed innocents in mass numbers to remove the possibility of these being turned into vampires and ended up with a rebellion wherein members of their own ranks began to willingly join the Tremere to take revenge on the &quot;Evil Wizards&quot;; Or, what if the mages tried to enchant the blood of the innocents to make them poisonous to vampires and instead ended up killing thousands of innocents... with the same results as above?; What if they tried to perform a great anti-vampire ritual that was disrupted by one Tremere noob using countermagick and the backlash opened up a gate to hell and killed enough elders that the Order wanted to bury that data? </div> </div> </div>If they did the dumb things you mentioned, more to my point, the OoH lost because they were dumb or incompetent. They didn't use their resources inteligently.<br /> <br /> Besides, why do you think any of these things would be believable?. None of them is:<br /> 1.<br /> &quot;killing inocents in mass numbers&quot; it's out of the posibility for both groups (and so it's mass embrace) because Humanity aren't the defensless sitting ducks you seem to believe. The Inquisition would have gutted them both if they attempted such a move (and other forces, like the Garou). And the rebelion would be part of the Tremere chronicles.<br /> 2.<br /> Enchanting the blood of inocents seems more like something OoH would do, but why would it make the blood poisonous?, Hermetics have a Pillar about life and healt, they know what's poisonous and what isn't. Plus, see 1.<br /> 3. Countermagic performed by A noob would never work on a mega-ritual by the rules because you have a limited dicepool and one roll. Plus, you have so many different rules and types of countermagic that you really should state what rules you're using, because not all counter is universal nor all work the same way. Study better your example instead of using the over-hype that countermagic has got lately.<br /> <br /> Finally, we can deduce that the strategies of the Order were stupid because we have the 2nd Massa War (where they used pretty bad strategies), and no reason to think that they were smarter before that now. On the contrary, if we get how powerful got the Tremere in modern age in comparisson with the DA, and how weak it's the OoH in comparisson with that time frame, and how both wars had similar results, I would say that the OoH had to be even more retarded at that age. <hr /><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Say for example, you are 10 mages who get together now and then. Each of you has a disciple mage and enough servants to support you so that you can perform your studies without needing to work. You note that one of your number has only been contacting you by mail for the past 100 years and think little of it until someone else brings up the possibility that that guy might be corrupted or something. The night after you bring this possibility to the leaders of your group, the group is beset by say 400 gargoyles supported by 100 vampires with countermagic as the first wave of the war. In the mean time the 18 mages have maybe only doubled in number to 36 or something. How is the larger scale war much different from this? Are these numbers off? Are there any problems with this scenario?<br /> <br /> As for rules, I'd say Vampire Dark Ages Vs. Mage Dark Ages would be the best fit for this sort of thing,</div> </div> </div> </div><i>I'm not sure what do you want to get with this</i>...but yes I have some problems with that scenario.<br /> <br /> A.<br /> 100 years it's like two or three generations of mages, stop thinking in &quot;vampiric time&quot;, it's preposterous to think nobody noticed vampiric infiltration and lived to tell the tale in two or three generations of mages that were born, grow, and died or reached Mastery, with how obvious vampires are. The easiest way would be to posit that the vampiric pressence wasn't very profound in the first 90 years or so (not what's in the books). You can attenuate this by saying that the conversion of House Tremere wasn't as complete or easy as the books would make one believe, that mostly was Ceoris and a few isolated Chantries, and that vampires never infiltrated Chantries instead using a few powerful Ghouls with masked aura and minds (doable with magick). Sadly, the accumen of mages at detecting the supernatural never get's mentioned, not even as something that should be overcomed with a clever strategy that we had to invent.<br /> Still 100, in Mage time, it's a LOOONG time for things to go south, you don't have to imagine that the apprentices fighting Tzimisce at the beggining were the same that discovered the Tremere plot and fleed.<br /> <br /> B. How didn't they saw that ambush you describe coming?. Splat with mandatory &quot;Auspex&quot;, among a bajillon of supernatural and natural means of getting information. Armies aren't easy to hide.<br /> <br /> C. Countermagic isn't that hot when your mages roll with two Traits, and also have countermagic agains disciplines (among other protections).<br /> <br /> E. Why, in your example, the Tremere get to have Gargoyles but the OoH don't get their Golems, Dragons and Angels + being in a fortress that was created to reppel mages, their supernatural constructs/minions, and mortals?<br /> <br /> D. If we got to that, 500 vampires vs 10 or so mages?, are you for real? Even with 1 mage per 1000 000 humans (mages are ludicrously rare), that's 10 vampires per mage, 100 vampires per each 10 mages would be more realistic. But, trully, 1 per 1000 000 never was right, and the OoH it's the most numerous mage group while the Tremere are one of the least numerous vampire group at that time, Gargoyles were never more common than them (as far as I know). If 32 mages, little more than 80 vampires counting Gargoyles would be believable. Also, see E (and 1 up there for the mass embrace).<br /> <br /> F. What where the Tzimisce doing while the Tremere send their forces away? I understand not aiding mages, but not attacking when your enemy it's distracted it's procrastination.<br /> <br /> G. the War was 80 years of skirmishes, your example it's an ambush, and thus fails to ilustrate this time. Again, don't think on &quot;vampire time&quot;, for the Tzimisce this may have been but a small breath of the Omen War, but for the Hermetics it's an entire life (or two) dedicated to war.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-18-2017, 02:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-18T14:16:06-07:00","id":1080552,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You didn't got my argument: We're discussing if it's realistic for the Tremere to have won the war.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, fine, forget my nonsense ideas with regards to that, my point is more, no book actually details what the Order did. Why are we just left with those option you mentioned? Why could not other things have happened?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If they did the dumb things you mentioned, more to my point, the OoH lost because they were dumb or incompetent. They didn't use their resources intelligently.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fine, I'll take the blame for these dumb ideas but just as a point of comparison, people do strange things in times of war like making incendiary bat bombs, trying to sneak estrogen into Hitler's food, and making pigeon guided missiles... and these are the winners of the war. Heck, people didn't even know at the time if the atom bomb would &quot;set the sky on fire&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Besides, why do you think any of these things would be believable?. None of them is:<br /> 1. &quot;killing inocents in mass numbers&quot; it's out of the posibility for both groups (and so it's mass embrace) because Humanity aren't the defensless sitting ducks you seem to believe. The Inquisition would have gutted them both if they attempted such a move (and other forces, like the Garou). And the rebelion would be part of the Tremere chronicles.</div> </div> </div> </div>So let's say it isn't. I won't argue this is believable, because I just kinda came up with it. My main thought is that solutions that might work on paper can screw up badly.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2. Enchanting the blood of inocents seems more like something OoH would do, but why would it make the blood poisonous?, Hermetics have a Pillar about life and healt, they know what's poisonous and what isn't. Plus, see 1.</div> </div> </div> </div>So you see no way of trying to give blood properties harmful to vampires can accidentally become harmful to people with a botch?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3. Countermagic performed by A noob would never work on a mega-ritual by the rules because you have a limited dicepool and one roll. Plus, you have so many different rules and types of countermagic that you really should state what rules you're using, because not all counter is universal nor all work the same way. Study better your example instead of using the over-hype that countermagic has got lately.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, you just have to make one caster screw up. That said, V20 Dark Ages seems to lack thaumaturgical countermagic for some reason.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Finally, we can deduce that the strategies of the Order were stupid because we have the 2nd Massa War (where they used pretty bad strategies), and no reason to think that they were smarter before that now. On the contrary, if we get how powerful got the Tremere in modern age in comparisson with the DA, and how weak it's the OoH in comparisson with that time frame, and how both wars had similar results, I would say that the OoH had to be even more retarded at that age.</div> </div> </div> </div>Could you elaborate on these stupid strategies?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>I'm not sure what do you want to get with this</i>...but yes I have some problems with that scenario.</div> </div> </div> </div>Before we get into the deeper parts of this, my intent is to set up an actual scenario that still runs with what the books say (That the majority can still agree on) that seems representative of the time and see how it pans out to see how far off the mark this actually is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A. 100 years it's like two or three generations of mages, stop thinking in &quot;vampiric time&quot;, it's preposterous to think nobody noticed vampiric infiltration and lived to tell the tale in two or three generations of mages that were born, grow, and died or reached Mastery, with how obvious vampires are.</div> </div> </div> </div>How obvious are Vampires? Also, why would they have to be perfectly hidden? Why couldn't the majority of Tremere have just been sold on becoming magickal immortal undead dudes to avoid the perceived loss of magick? Then the rest taken in by blood bond or living quite happily as awakened members of a partly vampiric house Tremere? You'd have the ocassional rebel for sure, but that could easily be taken care of. Then all you'd have to hide from really are the outsiders or occasional visitor from the other houses. Is there anything wrong with this idea?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">C. How didn't they saw that ambush coming?. Splat with mandatory &quot;Auspex&quot;, among a bajillon of supernatural and natural means of getting information. Armies aren't easy to hide.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd say no one was really looking until it was too late... but let's leave this stuff until we set up a better start to the scenario<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">B. Countermagic isn't that hot when your mages roll with two Traits, and also have countermagic agains disciplines (among other protections).</div> </div> </div> </div>Might have to discard countermagic altogether if it's not in the Dark Ages set<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">E. Why, in your example, the Tremere get to have Gargoyles but the OoH don't get their Golems, Dragons and Angels + being in a fortress that was created to reppel mages, their supernatural constructs/minions, and mortals?</div> </div> </div> </div>I never said they didn't<br /> Let's set it up better.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">D. If we got to that, 500 vampires vs 10 mages?, are you for real? Even with 1 mage per 1000 000 humans (mages are ludicrously rare), that's 10 vampires per mage, 100 vampires vs 10 mages would be more realistic. But, trully, 1 per 1000 000 never was right, and the OoH it's the most numerous mage group while the Tremere are one of the least numerous vampire group at that time. MAss embrace it's as much a statistical ilussion</div> </div> </div> </div>I just based my numbers off a crude, what if you could embrace x people per year and make x gargoyles per year.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">F. What where the Tzimisce doing while the Tremere send their forces away? I understand not aiding mages, but not attacking when your enemy it's distracted it's procrastination.</div> </div> </div> </div>So let's see how they fit in<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080415#post1080415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">G. the War was 80 years of skirmishes, your example it's an ambush, and thus fails to ilustrate this time. Again, don't think on &quot;vampire time&quot;, for the Tzimisce this may have been but a small breath of the Omen War, but for the Hermetics it's an entire life (or two) dedicated to war.</div> </div> </div> </div>My example was as the first wave of battle. but let me get this down in a better way.<br /> <br /> (Sorry for the long post)<br /> <br /> <br /> I would presume that the conversion of house Tremere went pretty well, with the majority becoming vampires of their own will after early developments in Thaumaturgy bore enough fruit to seem promising (within 2 years supposedly) and most of the rest were either cool with it or blood bound. This means a united Tremere was able to prep for the inevitable war. (Actually, I say they should have just brought their findings to the Order and become the Order's Vampire house, but that's unlikely to have worked as some Houses seem to always be looking for an excuse to make war on one another)<br /> <br /> In the meantime, the rest of the Order were, well, I guess trying to figure out the problem of Paradox and the failure of certain previously reliable magickal effects. General spying on the Tremere probably got anyone about as much info as it did on the other Houses and couldn't really be used to condemn them as it was against the law. I honestly presume that the Tremere really set themselves up in a far remote location to put themselves out of mind of the Order.<br /> <br /> To have this make any sense we will need to start this off with what a typical setup for a mage House would be like.<br /> Let's Assume Dark Ages rules for both mages and vampires.<br /> <br /> Let as assume that each house is led by a Mage with a Foundation at 5 and a pillar at 5 with maybe other pillars at 4.<br /> This Mage has maybe a 5 loyal disciples with a Foundation of 4 and a pillar at 4 maybe other pillars at 3.<br /> Let's assume each of these has 5 Disciples with a Foundation of 3 and a pillar at 3 maybe other pillars at 2.<br /> Let's assume each of these has 5 Disciples with a Foundation of 2 and a pillar at 2 maybe other pillars at 1.<br /> <br /> So that's 1 head of a House, +5 Masters, +25 Students + 125 noobs. (156 awakened?)<br /> It's unlikely they all lived in one place, but whatever the case, they would likely have been served by a community or numerous communities adding up to maybe 3000 (a town) if we considered them something like the nobles of their little domain.<br /> <br /> Let's assume the Order has 10 of something like these and progresses at its usual pace.<br /> The Head would remain the head, the masters would have maybe tripled in number by promoting 2 students each to their ranks and these students would get students with students to get something like 1 Head, 15 masters, 75 students, and 375 noobs (466 per house x 10, for a total mage population of 4660 in the Order served by maybe 20 towns all over Europe of 3000 each at this point)<br /> <br /> Let's say most of Tremere becomes Vampires early on to arrive at<br /> 1 Head Vampire, +4 Master Vampires, +20 Vampiric Students, +100 Young vampires (125 Vampires)<br /> 1 Master Mage, +5 Students, + 25 noob mages (for 31 mages). I'm guessing mage recruitment stagnated around this point.<br /> Vampire recruitment can go so rapidly that we have to take into account the fact that vampires can outstrip a land's ability to sustain them.<br /> <br /> According to a 2008 statement 10,000,000 people go missing per year in the U.S. and out 500,000 are never found (out of a 304 million U.S. population)<br /> So if 500,000 out of 304,000,000 million can disappear each year (0.16% of a population) in a modern country without raising alarm bells.<br /> Transylvania at the time with a population that should be larger than 349,000 should easily accommodate the disappearance of 558 people a year.<br /> (This is a low estimate made in the 1200s after the mongols invaded)<br /> While this would suggest that you could have 55800 Tremere Vampires at the end of this, that would mean they'd need too many people to make this work.<br /> If they became something like the secret lords of Transylvania, controlling all communication and ruling the country with an iron fist, they'd probably be limited to around 5% (17450 Vampires including Gargoyles), though this would also mean that everyone in Transylvania would know of the problem (Well, Vampires are a thing there so maybe everyone did know)<br /> This I assume is when it became impossible to hide what they were doing and the war kinda started at this point, with the Order startled at the revelation and the Tremere already ready.<br /> <br /> I'd like to go into stats and microcosmic versions of the battle but that would be premature. Let's see what everyone thinks I got right or wrong here. Also I have to eat.<br /> <br /> <br /> Feel free to criticize any or all my assumptions<br /> I love this forum. I'm never bored.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-19T08:08:22-07:00","id":1080903,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1080552#post1080552\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love this forum. I'm never bored.</div> </div> </div> </div>That thought crossed my mind several times.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>So, this Zen Master walks up to a hot dog stand and says: &quot;Make me one with everything!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12022-nonsense","name":"Nonsense","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"815"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-19T12:08:05-07:00","id":1081020,"title":"","content":"Looking at this set up, considering the sort of powers the heads of each house could bring to bear according to this model (using a Foundation and a Pillar at 5), I'm beginning to think that the supposed 100 mages who survived the war were simply the Heads of each house and their favorite disciples and that promotion into the upper echelon of magehood of each house was a bit less than half the speed I had imagined. That would make for 100 people with a Foundation of 4+ and a pillar at 4+. The vampires just couldn't kill them and had to settle for killing everybody else. Then perhaps the mages retaliated and wiped out the parts of house Tremere they could find, though Tremere, knowing the Order's methods were able to preserve enough of their resources to turn this into some long bloody overextended war. But that's just an initial assessment.<br /> <br /> So unless, anyone has something to add to the above I'm going to now try and figure out what sorts of weapons the mages and vampires could bring to bear and in what numbers.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-20T00:20:36-07:00","id":1081344,"title":"","content":"To me the numbers seem a bit high.<br /> It is just a hunch, though.<br /> Probably the numbers are off because only growth is accounted for. Some mages/vampires ought to fall to age/twilight(quiet)/accidents/hunters/rivals/whathaveyou.<br /> Additionally the Reign over Transylvania itches me as improbable, if not impossible.<br /> <br /> What crossed my mind was the idea that the Tremere are much more centralized, and the OoH more widespread.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>So, this Zen Master walks up to a hot dog stand and says: &quot;Make me one with everything!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12022-nonsense","name":"Nonsense","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"815"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-20T11:54:35-07:00","id":1081516,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nonsense</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081344#post1081344\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To me the numbers seem a bit high.<br /> It is just a hunch, though.<br /> Probably the numbers are off because only growth is accounted for. Some mages/vampires ought to fall to age/twilight(quiet)/accidents/hunters/rivals/whathaveyou.<br /> Additionally the Reign over Transylvania itches me as improbable, if not impossible.<br /> <br /> What crossed my mind was the idea that the Tremere are much more centralized, and the OoH more widespread.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay. Well, I'll take a hint from the 100 survivors thing too and see how this works if the masters only promote one mage each to their number in a century.<br /> That would give us 1 head, 10 masters, 50 students, and 250 newbies per house at the end of this for a total of 311 per house of 3110 members of the Order<br /> <br /> As for Tremere, we use the same starting point and rationale to arrive at<br /> 2 masters, 10 Students, &amp; 50 newbies for a total of 62 mages.<br /> As for the 17450, that does assume that they did take over and were pretty much lording over the place. I'll address that in a moment.<br /> I have more accurate population numbers now. 349,000 is supposedly somewhere between 85% to 50% of what the the population was before the Mongols invaded. So, let's say it is 67% and assume that the population at the time was 520896. This would give a maximum of (5%) 26044 vampires (including gargoyles) assuming they lorded over the place, which as you pointed out seems improbable. My wonder is how far they'd be able to go. Presumably, they'd push as far as they could in preparation for war. I'll arbitrarily cut the number to 1/3 of this and assume that these numbers were enough to scare the local populace and the powers that be and raise the suspicions of the Order of Hermes in earnest. (Not acting like Vampires so to speak). That would make 8681 Vampires. I'm really open to suggestions here. Also, I have no idea at this point of the ratio of Tremere vampires to Gargoyles.<br /> <br /> This suggests that each mage of the Order would be outnumbered 3 to 1 by vampires in the best of circumstances and were unlikely to ever meet a Tremere Mage if there were any still left at the time. As suggested, it's likely the Tremere could have concentrated their forces for an even higher numeric advantage, though with some risk of facing a prepared foe after the first battle.<br /> <br /> <br /> I would put the mage capabilities as something like this:<br /> <br /> Head of House: One of: Transform Vampires into inanimate objects; Transform Vampires into mindless corpse; Kill opposing forces with natural disaster; plus everything their lessers can do<br /> Masters: One of: Transform Vampires into Mundane Animal; Transform Vampires into lackey; Create Real Sunlight; plus everything their lessers can do<br /> Students: One of: Heal injuries; Confuse Vampires; Counter Thaumaturgy; Fight using elemental superpowers; plus everything their lessers can do<br /> Newbies: Enhance their mundane fighting capabilities &amp; use Supernatural Senses<br /> <br /> This is obviously quite generalized but based on my reading of the Dark ages powers.<br /> <br /> <br /> Tremere Vampires of the highest levels would likely be able to: Astral Project, Possess a Mortal Creature,<br /> and one of: Attack using a Blazing Inferno; Telekinetically Toss 1000 lbs; Summon a powerful elemental; Instill Self Loathing<br /> Plus whatever their lessers can do.<br /> <br /> Their Students are liable to be able to: Read Minds, Reshape Memories, Lob bolts of fire, Animate an object, Sow Dissent, Induce Malady,<br /> plus a smattering of lesser abilities including fast travel &amp; inhuman senses and all the standard vampire powers.<br /> <br /> New Vampires will likely tend to focus on one of these powers a student would have and also have a smattering of lesser abilities and all the standard vampire powers.<br /> <br /> <br /> Still have to figure out what gargolyles can do but looking at this, it does seem like the Master Mages will mop the floor with the &quot;Master&quot; Vampires, whereas in a battle between students, the vampires will tend to have the upper hand due to numbers and newbie mages will almost certainly have problems when outnumbered by young vampires... but that's just my opinion.<br /> <br /> Note that this would just be the first wave of battle.<br /> I'll keep going with this until a cleared view of the war comes out.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-20T15:47:10-07:00","id":1081609,"title":"","content":"I personally think it's partially bullshit that the hermetics lost. It was necessary for sales of vampire to have the hermetics loose<br /> <br /> You can't sell your flagship line as well if the vampires who people often play keep getting shit kicked! <br /> <br /> The hermetics get shoe horned into whatever they need to be to have lost the war. However they need to get rewritten, they get rewritten, that's the bottom line in this.<br /> <br /> Logistically speaking, the whole idea that it would take about two centuries for the order to learn the Tremere were vampires also strikes me as bullshit but it's what the vampire authors decided to write so it had to be cannon in mage.<br /> <br /> It is my opinion that the most sensible thing to do is to retcon it all, it's what I do. Say that from the hermetic perspective, the order found out about the Tremere transformation, held a council, banished the Tremere from the order and shamed them (which Tremere could give two shits about) and then said &quot;all of your nodes belong to us&quot;.<br /> <br /> The Tremere said &quot;fuck you&quot; (on principle) even thought they could no longer use the nodes and a brief skirmish was fought for the most valuable sources of quintessence which were either claimed or moved by the order, after which they signed a non aggression pact with Tremere and said &quot;we are too high and mighty to waste our time with mere vampires so we'll show the world how noble we are and let them live out of pity&quot; which was hermetic code for &quot;they reproduce like rabbits and we awakened are like rare pearls so let's not risk our apprentices in a potentially costly battle&quot;.<br /> <br /> The Tremere said the same thing but for different reasons. &quot;we will not waste our time on the pathetic fools living in the past who have not realized that vampirism is the future and magedom is an obsolete past, let them die of old age while we amass power instead of risking our apprentices in a potentially costly battle&quot;.<br /> <br /> Then both sides went their separate ways and wrote their own histories afterwards","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3148,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","user":"Zennis","date":". 04-20-2017, 09:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","name":"Zennis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-20T16:42:58-07:00","id":1081625,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081516#post1081516\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll keep going with this until a cleared view of the war comes out.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have to remark about your analysis:<br /> -The number of Mages seem quite important in regard to the total population, what was the ratio supposed to be at that time? (number of Tremere is also high, but bloodsucking rabbit &amp; stuff maybe?)<br /> -You didn't mention Perdo Magica in the abilities of the Tremer which is odd because if I remember well they pretty much created it for this (and it got useless after)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5462-kendaan","name":"Kendaan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-21T06:41:21-07:00","id":1081810,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kendaan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081625#post1081625\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have to remark about your analysis:<br /> -The number of Mages seem quite important in regard to the total population, what was the ratio supposed to be at that time? (number of Tremere is also high, but bloodsucking rabbit &amp; stuff maybe?)</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, mage mentions numbers like 1 in a million or 1 in 50,000 to 25,000 but I always assumed those to be modern estimates and not exactly relevant to the dark ages. I was under the assumption that there were considerably more of them relative to the population back then, especially since Europe's population was around 62 million at the time.<br /> <br /> As for my Tremere numbers, the 5% was based on numbers of the rough ruling class to commoner ratio at the time and I'm assuming that to be the point where it becomes blatantly obvious that vampires run the country. At that point or higher, there would be no real way to hide the Tremere in Transylvania. The actual number should be less than this although that would also assume that the Tremere didn't spread outside Transylvania.<br /> <br /> Would you have any specific suggestions with regards to numbers?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kendaan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081625#post1081625\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-You didn't mention Perdo Magica in the abilities of the Tremer which is odd because if I remember well they pretty much created it for this (and it got useless after)</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't mention it because it seems to be completely absent in V20 Dark ages for some odd reason...<br /> The addition of it would make things more dangerous for the mages but wouldn't actually assure victory against Masters and Archmagi.<br /> <br /> 10 dice of countermagic to cancel a power or ritual that targets anything within a radius equal to his Willpower in yards or meters, or one that is being used in that distance would only be available to a handful of vampires. While it could be used to shutdown archmagi or master, this would assume the vampire can actually get within that distance of an archmagi before he or she creates a glowing orb that emits sunlight or summons a natural disaster. So while it makes it possible for the vampires to win against powerful mages, it in no way assures victory against the crazy tricks that an ancient an paranoid mage would usually have. On the other hand, 6 dice of countermagic against magic that targets a vampire or something touching the vampire would certainly be a serious problem for most Order of Hermes student mages and newbies.<br /> <br /> It is my opinion that the initial attack against the Order of Hermes by the Tremere mostly killed the younger and less experienced mages of the order and much of their mortal compatriots (and maybe a few older ones here and there) but cost the Tremere dearly. It would have left a disorganized and greatly reduced Order of Hermes, however.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-21T12:02:55-07:00","id":1081949,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">So you see no way of trying to give blood properties harmful to vampires can accidentally become harmful to people with a botch?</div> </div> </div> </div>If you botch, there's a Backlash. I guess a devious Storyteller could decide that the Backlash, instead of taking the usual forms, would take the form of turning the ritual harmful to others, but these things are mostly a ST fiat.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Could you elaborate on these stupid strategies? </div> </div> </div>They were discussed at the thread I mentioned-a good example it's the general idea that attacking vampiric fortresses when they're awake (at night) it's somehow a good idea.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How obvious are Vampires? </div> </div> </div>A lot. There's a reason why the Camarilla discourages the interaction with mortals. Frenzy alone it's a good reason to never enter in contact with huge (or powerful) groups of mortals that may consider killing you if you start hissing and popping fangs. Then you have the fact that mages know a lot and are very perceptive, you don't want to make a fuck-up near them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, why would they have to be perfectly hidden? </div> </div> </div>Mortal Tremere were fighting against the Tzimisce in the beginning of the Omen War (by RAW), this solves the problem of how a group of neonates survived the Tzimisce, and thus I applaud that choice. However, it makes the need to hidde the vampires more important. I hate that the creators of vampire thought important to make clear how the Tremere survived against Elders, but didn't thought important to tell us how they didn't got discovered by mages (who, again, are very perceptive)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why couldn't the majority of Tremere have just been sold on becoming magickal immortal undead dudes to avoid the perceived loss of magick?<br /> .<br /> .<br /> .<br /> I would presume that the conversion of house Tremere went pretty well, with the majority becoming vampires of their own will </div> </div> </div>This it's not very likely. You see, the anguish that mages feel when they loose their magick it's beyond the mere loss of &quot;power&quot;. It's the loss of your Avatar, and with it, the extraordinary perception of Reality that Awakening gives Mages. It's like being both blind and castrated. The vague promise of the transcendence of Ascencion it's gone and now have a Beast pushing you to sub-humanity. It's said that suicide it's common among Gilguled, and so it's among mages turned vampire. This it's the drama of the vampire turned mage (can't recall where I read this, but I remember it from various Mage sources). It's a big drama, no Mage want's to be &quot;Gilguled&quot;, some may be old and desperate enough to try it, like Tremere, but they should be a minority.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> early developments in Thaumaturgy bore enough fruit to seem promising (within 2 years supposedly) </div> </div> </div>I still can't cope with the Thaumaturgy &quot;the esoteric discipline by excelence&quot;, &quot;the discipline that has to be teached because merely drinking the blood of a Tremere wouldn't be enough&quot; was created in two years...I can totally believe that it would be much, much, faster to invent such a Discipline for Tremere, who was a rockstar at magick (a Master), and had a clan researching with him, than for joe, the average sorcerer. But heck, that was fast. Some books took much longer to write than that. <hr /><br /> About for your new scenary, it's better than the first approach, but:<br /> <br /> You need to account for the Tzimisce, and how much can they put on the table.<br /> <br /> It's important to note that the fact this was, as most wars between supernaturals, a Shadow War. The major advantage of vampires at a shadow war it's their skill to manipulate society, while the advantage that mages have in this regards it's their skill to gather knowledge (to fight a shadow war, misdirection it's the key) and pull off weird shenanigans (Thaumaturgy can also do the latter, albeit at a smaller pace than Magick)<br /> <br /> Plus, unless you're inventing a new war, it was the Hermetics who attacked (this had advantages and dissadvantages for the OoH. The advantage it's prep time (this it's why I find hard to buy that mages were entirely ignorant of vampires when they got there). The disadvantage it's that you don't get to fight in your super-warded castle Chantry, but have to fight against the walls and Wards of the Tremere.<br /> Idealy Hermetics would attack by day, so you mostly need to fight wards, ghouls (some of which could be mage, so it's not as simple as mages would like), and Tremere who waked up thanks to the common ritual that does that when someone get's close). By night Tremere would search for mages to kill them, and mages would need to set a camp either well hidden or with good supernatural protections (here it's where the tenuous aliance of the Order with Tzimisce could come in handy, as voivodes know the land and could provide shelter in towns that are within their domains, where the Tremere would have trouble trying to enter). Mages have a little advantage in the sleeping departament because they can use magick to function without sleep, but even then, running on magick alone for a long time may have side effects.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It is my opinion that the initial attack against the Order of Hermes by the Tremere mostly killed the younger and less experienced mages of the order and much of their mortal compatriots (and maybe a few older ones here and there) but cost the Tremere dearly. It would have left a disorganized and greatly reduced Order of Hermes, however. </div> </div> </div>I can see why the war would be more difficult for young members of both groups (mainly because youngsters are the more likely to actualy &quot;be&quot; in the conflict)<br /> However, instead of trying to justify the number given in OoH Trad. Book (that was IOC info that could have been given to discourage young mages, as someone stated earlier), it may be better to think that the Order withdraw before loosing a critical number (let's say, before loosing a 50%). The Tremere were trully in no position to surrender in any war, as they were besieged by their enemies, but the OoH could retreat (knowing that the Tremere wouldn't pursue).<br /> <br /> <br /> EDIT: On the Tremere beneffit, I would say that (at least at the beginning) probably most Gangrel, Nosferatu and Tzimisce can't tell the difference between an uppity group of usurper mages in robes and the mages in robes that want to kill the usurper mages. Meanwhile Hermetics probably would think (again, at the beginning) that all vampires are Tremere creations just like the Gargoyles","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-21-2017, 03:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-21T12:31:29-07:00","id":1081961,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would put the mage capabilities as something like this:<br /> <br /> Head of House: One of: Transform Vampires into inanimate objects; Transform Vampires into mindless corpse; Kill opposing forces with natural disaster; plus everything their lessers can do<br /> Masters: One of: Transform Vampires into Mundane Animal; Transform Vampires into lackey; Create Real Sunlight; plus everything their lessers can do<br /> Students: One of: Heal injuries; Confuse Vampires; Counter Thaumaturgy; Fight using elemental superpowers; plus everything their lessers can do<br /> Newbies: Enhance their mundane fighting capabilities &amp; use Supernatural Senses<br /> <br /> This is obviously quite generalized but based on my reading of the Dark ages powers.<br /> <br /> <br /> Tremere Vampires of the highest levels would likely be able to: Astral Project, Possess a Mortal Creature,<br /> and one of: Attack using a Blazing Inferno; Telekinetically Toss 1000 lbs; Summon a powerful elemental; Instill Self Loathing<br /> Plus whatever their lessers can do.<br /> <br /> Their Students are liable to be able to: Read Minds, Reshape Memories, Lob bolts of fire, Animate an object, Sow Dissent, Induce Malady,<br /> plus a smattering of lesser abilities including fast travel &amp; inhuman senses and all the standard vampire powers.</div> </div> </div> </div>This method...isn't very convincing. The thing with mages it's that they're Dynamic, giving them a few preffered techniques (one of which it's a long standing joke) isn't a good way to represent them. It does a discervice to Thaumaturgical rituals too.<br /> <br /> As a more holistic approach, if you want to persist, could be to rethink the strategies after a few &quot;turns&quot; of the war (probably a &quot;turn&quot; could be three months or so). Mages get to &quot;adapt&quot; (rethink the strategy to something that would better counter the other party) two or three times per each time vampire does to represent their great &quot;dynamicity&quot;<br /> <br /> While you want to be purist in V20 DA, I would suggest to add the Path Countermagic after a turn or two (It isn't in V20 DA, but it exists in V20, and this particular conflict it's the best moment to develop and teach such a rare Path).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-21T12:36:09-07:00","id":1081964,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081810#post1081810\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Would you have any specific suggestions with regards to numbers?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>The number you had would be about 0.5%, I think 1 per 1000 would be more accurate, so that'd be around 500?<br /> <br /> As for the Tremere, I'd say at best twice as much (not counting Gargoyles) as they were specific of whom they embracebut selected quite a few apprentices rejected by the Order from what I've read","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5462-kendaan","name":"Kendaan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-21T14:47:43-07:00","id":1082014,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081949#post1081949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">it's the general idea that attacking vampiric fortresses when they're awake (at night) it's somehow a good idea.</div> </div> </div> </div>That does sound stupid. Sorry, could you tell me where that's from?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081949#post1081949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A lot. There's a reason why the Camarilla discourages the interaction with mortals. Frenzy alone it's a good reason to never enter in contact with huge (or powerful) groups of mortals that may consider killing you if you start hissing and popping fangs. Then you have the fact that mages know a lot and are very perceptive, you don't want to make a fuck-up near them.</div> </div> </div> </div>With regards to perceptive, I've found that mages are pretty good at finding stuff they are looking for and only somewhat better than mortals at finding stuff they aren't looking for. So if mage &quot;X&quot; is looking for the secrets of the heavens and the vampires are running around in another country at night, how would the mage &quot;X&quot; actually find them?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081949#post1081949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This it's not very likely. You see, the anguish that mages feel when they loose their magick it's beyond the mere loss of &quot;power&quot;. It's the loss of your Avatar, and with it, the extraordinary perception of Reality that Awakening gives Mages. It's like being both blind and castrated. The vague promise of the transcendence of Ascencion it's gone and now have a Beast pushing you to sub-humanity. It's said that suicide it's common among Gilguled, and so it's among mages turned vampire. This it's the drama of the vampire turned mage (can't recall where I read this, but I remember it from various Mage sources). It's a big drama, no Mage want's to be &quot;Gilguled&quot;, some may be old and desperate enough to try it, like Tremere, but they should be a minority.</div> </div> </div> </div>It would be nice to get a source for this. Even so, they don't feel this before it happens and the Vampire Tremere, perhaps wanting others to accompany them in their misery could probably easily discount pain as &quot;the pain of death&quot; and make excuses for suddenly missing Tremere who committed suicide. The elders could also wipe such things from memory.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081949#post1081949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I still can't cope with the Thaumaturgy &quot;the esoteric discipline by excelence&quot;, &quot;the discipline that has to be teached because merely drinking the blood of a Tremere wouldn't be enough&quot; was created in two years...I can totally believe that it would be much, much, faster to invent such a Discipline for Tremere, who was a rockstar at magick (a Master), and had a clan researching with him, than for joe, the average sorcerer. But heck, that was fast. Some books took much longer to write than that. <hr /></div> </div> </div> </div>I'd say two years is a long time if the base material already exists. I'm guessing the 1st thing they'd try is finding something within them that is like an avatar within them. Likely, all they'd find is the beast. Then they'd try their numerous techniques for applying enlightened will to it. This probably wouldn't do much. Then you'd apply all manner of techniques for applying gnostic techniques, treating the beast as their divine spark. If that didn't work they'd then try a perverse from of Theurgy, treating the beast as a separate spiritual entity. Failing thus, they'd then try to find another source of supernatural power withing themselves and then try applying the same enlightened, gnostic, and theurgical techniques. They'd probably try it using the blood, the humors, the heart, and then other esoteric things. Once they found the source of power they could mess with, they'd actually have most of the rest of the instructions as part of their gnostic heritage hedge magic heritage, with a few substitutions necessary her and there. The reason merely drinking the blood of a Tremere won't give you Thaumaturgy is because you have to learn it in the manner of learning from a tutor or a book. It's counter-intuitive... like higher math or like physics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081949#post1081949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You need to account for the Tzimisce, and how much can they put on the table.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, I was planing to try and fit them in later, after the Tremere and Order were more or less solid, so we can see if the whole model sinks or swims.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081949#post1081949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's important to note that the fact this was, as most wars between supernaturals, a Shadow War. The major advantage of vampires at a shadow war it's their skill to manipulate society, while the advantage that mages have in this regards it's their skill to gather knowledge (to fight a shadow war, misdirection it's the key) and pull off weird shenanigans (Thaumaturgy can also do the latter, albeit at a smaller pace than Magick)</div> </div> </div> </div>Would you suggest then more specifically how the Hermetics would have handled things? Without outside input, my ideas will be quite narrow.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081949#post1081949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Plus, unless you're inventing a new war, it was the Hermetics who attacked (this had advantages and dissadvantages for the OoH. The advantage it's prep time (this it's why I find hard to buy that mages were entirely ignorant of vampires when they got there). The disadvantage it's that you don't get to fight in your super-warded castle Chantry, but have to fight against the walls and Wards of the Tremere.</div> </div> </div> </div>Please note, my model only encompasses stage 1 of the war and not even completely at that. Admittedly my comments about the strongest 100 mages are not actual to the complete model of the war and only suggested by a total bashfest between the 2 groups.<br /> <br /> So with regards to attack, the reason I'm going under the assumption the Tremere attacked first is because the Tremere were the ones expecting this war.<br /> So the mages found out, gathered in force, and struck at the Tremere Castle... and the Tremere knew 100 years ago that this would happen. What would they do. What would you do?<br /> I'd leave some guys there to stall the mages (hoping they didn't decide to just nuke the place) while I went off to destroy your now less protected holdings. I would try to make the castle as much of a death trap to as many mages and mage resources as possible but I would not use it as my base. That would be some magically hidden hiding hole somewhere else. Then while they're busy with that, I'd go for whatever they left behind and set forth those dragons the books mention.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-21T15:09:25-07:00","id":1082019,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081961#post1081961\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This method...isn't very convincing. The thing with mages it's that they're Dynamic, giving them a few preffered techniques (one of which it's a long standing joke) isn't a good way to represent them. It does a discervice to Thaumaturgical rituals too.</div> </div> </div> </div>These are broad generalizations. I eventually actually plan to run and roll combat and less direct battles between mages and vampires as part of trying to figure this out, using specific rotes and thaumaturgical applications. I'm trying to establish a rough idea of numbers first.<br /> <br /> If you have any applications of thaumaturgy or awakened magic you think I'm overlooking, please point them out and I'll see how to add them.<br /> <br /> My comment about how the battle might go before we even started is just made because a mage with Vires 4+ who knows anything about vampires will probably start making flying glowy orbs of true sunlight at a great distance and be done with the vampires fairly quick. What i haven't found is a way for the younger mages to deal with larger numbers of supernatural opponents. Throw in Countermagic to the mix and you really have a scenario where the younger mages are at a terrible disadvantage while the older ones are still nearly unbeatable unless taken by surprise.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081961#post1081961\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a more holistic approach, if you want to persist, could be to rethink the strategies after a few &quot;turns&quot; of the war (probably a &quot;turn&quot; could be three months or so). Mages get to &quot;adapt&quot; (rethink the strategy to something that would better counter the other party) two or three times per each time vampire does to represent their great &quot;dynamicity&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>This sounds good. I'm still ironing out turn 1 though. I have no idea how long turns will last but I suspect when the next turn happens will have a lot to do with how the each turn ends.<br /> <br /> So far, turn I guess I'll set turn 1 as mages go and assault the Tremere castle, while the Tremere try to assault the Order strongholds.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081961#post1081961\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While you want to be purist in V20 DA, I would suggest to add the Path Countermagic after a turn or two (It isn't in V20 DA, but it exists in V20, and this particular conflict it's the best moment to develop and teach such a rare Path).</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay. I will do this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12480,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","user":"Spacecat","date":". 04-21-2017, 06:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-21T15:12:22-07:00","id":1082021,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kendaan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1081964#post1081964\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The number you had would be about 0.5%, I think 1 per 1000 would be more accurate, so that'd be around 500?<br /> <br /> As for the Tremere, I'd say at best twice as much (not counting Gargoyles) as they were specific of whom they embracebut selected quite a few apprentices rejected by the Order from what I've read</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry, you lost me. 500 what? (Did I screw up my math somewhere?)<br /> <br /> How many Tremere, Gargoyles, &amp; Hermetics?<br /> <br /> <br /> Edit: I'm currently designing mages of varying levels of power and capability and vampires and gargoyles and such... and reading up on their capabilities. This may take a while. Th numbers on each side though will have great bearing on the tactics they can use.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12480,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","user":"Spacecat","date":". 04-22-2017, 04:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-23T11:48:14-07:00","id":1082725,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">That does sound stupid. Sorry, could you tell me where that's from?</div> </div> </div> </div>Blood Treachery.<br /> <br /> As mark puts it in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/379957-how-anyone-tried-out-a-hermetic-tremere-war\">the old Thread that theorizes about just the kind of stuff you're thinking right now</a>:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark, in that old thread</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">5. &quot;ok here's our battle plan we charge in the middle of the night with guns through the front door. no research no plan no preparation no backup no umbrood summon . and absolutely no magick! because obviously 3 points of paradox are worse than being killed!...even if magic is coincidental in their chantries.</div> </div> </div> </div>(you should read the whole rant, it's rather funny)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark, in that old thread</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">then again even this book makes more sense than the first masassa war. with the tremere hiding vampirism for 177 years and surviving agains tzimisce gangrel warrior salubri and hermetics all the while they are in their infancy. i mean seriously the entire thing should have ended in a month or so. the order literally outdotted them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Latter the thread goes on discussing if that it's true or not (albeit a lot it's about modern days). This is why I think it's relevant to link it here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> With regards to perceptive, I've found that mages are pretty good at finding stuff they are looking for and only somewhat better than mortals at finding stuff they aren't looking for. So if mage &quot;X&quot; is looking for the secrets of the heavens and the vampires are running around in another country at night, how would the mage &quot;X&quot; actually find them? </div> </div> </div><ul><li>While searching for Tzimisce and other vampires in or near the Chantry</li> <li>Mages in the chantry B had the great idea of putting a ward against vampires (a stationary effect that detects undead resonance and lack of life should be doable with Vires Primus and Anima), then a vampiric Tremere from chantry A (which had been ashed by Tzimisce) enters running asking for asylum.</li> <li>Mortal Tremere with Awareness 5 that has been fighting vampires for some time cassualy gets near a vampire Tremere and get's the &quot;vampiric vive&quot;.She decides to investigate.</li> <li>Trembley the vampiric Tremere takes a strol to find something to drink, and just then he passes by Dufus the Clumsy, which accidentally drops a torch to the foot of Trembley. Then nature takes it's course and either Dufus it's bruttaly murdered or manages to beat Trembley and now he has reasons to be suspicious.</li> <li>A Tremere, hating his unlife more than the usual, decides to tell the tale regardless of the fact he's going to be killed by the Order.</li> <li>A vampire hunter of the Inquisition finds himself pursuing a massa that it's protected by of a group of heathen sorcerers. He manages to get in by day, but it's captured and interrogated. Obviously his affirmations are those of a madman, but then, vampiric have started to show more and more within our chantries...so it's worth to search.</li> <li>Same as above, but with Tzimisce. Man, why everyone insists that our ranks are filled with undead?.</li> <li>Rivals of the Old Faith/Messianic Voices discover the rouse while attacking Tremere, then orchestrate a way to reveal the data to the Order, after all, an enemy that fights with himself it's weakening itself</li> <li>Ned the Tremere neonate tries to feed on a woman but it's careless and doesn't realize that he's been spied by Judas, the Treacheros Tytalus, that was spying tremere to get brive material, who proceeds to read his mind to discover that this wasn't just a perverted mage, but a member of a widespread conspiracy</li> <li>Same than the above, but this time the Tytalan send a spirit to get information of his enemies within the rival House. He surely get his info, just it's scarier than he expected.</li> <li>Should I continue?</li> </ul><br /> My point there it's not to proove that it's impossible for the Tremere to have been hidden for 100 years to the Order as a whole (I know that all of the above can be averted), but to highlight that, in all those years, it's unrealisticto think NOTHING got wrong. NO Tremere fell to the Beast in a bad moment, no spy was sucessful, no one of those mages that were fighting in the beginings of the Omen War was savvy enough to search for vampires in their midst.<br /> I'm more willing to believe that the &quot;masquerade had his accidents&quot;. A group of the Tremere did know, but decided not to tell, thinking that the corruption wouldn't be so big as to reach Ceoris, while others knew, but hadn't enough proof to call a tribunal, or didn't wanted to get caught in the middle of a war(and thus decided to lay low and avoid capture). I think that at least a chapter about this in some book would be appreciated. Otherwise mages seem just sitting ducks<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It would be nice to get a source for this. Even so, they don't feel this before it happens and the Vampire Tremere, perhaps wanting others to accompany them in their misery could probably easily discount pain as &quot;the pain of death&quot; and make excuses for suddenly missing Tremere who committed suicide. The elders could also wipe such things from memory. </div> </div> </div>Any Tremere old enough to consent being killed to become immortal should be savvy (and, likely, powerful) enough to at least research what would happen.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm guessing the 1st thing they'd try is finding something within them that is like an avatar within them. Likely, all they'd find is the beast. Then they'd try their numerous techniques for applying enlightened will to it. This probably wouldn't do much. Then you'd apply all manner of techniques for applying gnostic techniques, treating the beast as their divine spark. If that didn't work they'd then try a perverse from of Theurgy, treating the beast as a separate spiritual entity. Failing thus, they'd then try to find another source of supernatural power withing themselves and then try applying the same enlightened, gnostic, and theurgical techniques. They'd probably try it using the blood, the humors, the heart, and then other esoteric things. Once they found the source of power they could mess with, they'd actually have most of the rest of the instructions as part of their gnostic heritage hedge magic heritage, with a few substitutions necessary her and there. </div> </div> </div>And doing all of that it's literally a lot easier than solving fermat's last theorem?, writing 1 Game of Thrones book takes more than developing the first Thaumaturgical grimoire?. Inventing Thaumaturgy itself it's easier than getting a phd?. Then how long takes to create a Path?, one month?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So with regards to attack, the reason I'm going under the assumption the Tremere attacked first is because the Tremere were the ones expecting this war.<br /> So the mages found out, gathered in force, and struck at the Tremere Castle... and the Tremere knew 100 years ago that this would happen. What would they do. What would you do?<br /> I'd leave some guys there to stall the mages (hoping they didn't decide to just nuke the place) while I went off to destroy your now less protected holdings. I would try to make the castle as much of a death trap to as many mages and mage resources as possible but I would not use it as my base. That would be some magically hidden hiding hole somewhere else. Then while they're busy with that, I'd go for whatever they left behind and set forth those dragons the books mention. </div> </div> </div>You should get into the setting. This wasn't two castles in nearby mountains. The Order of Hermes was spread trough Europe. Meanwhile, the Tremere had become more insular. The Tremere were besieged by the Tzimisce, Gangrel, Nosferatu, and remaining Salubri.<br /> <br /> In order to do what you imagine, the Tremere should have moved armies of blood sucking undead trough Europe. This it's a logistical nightmare, how are you going to provide the blood for the whole army and at the same time not attract the attention?, how are you going to move arround massive numbers of vampires trough the Domain of the undead Princes without making all the vampires in the world declare war at you?, how are you going to do all that unnoticed by other supernatural forces that hunt vampires?.<br /> <br /> Mages are easier to move arround, because they are less, they can travel during day like everyone else, and drink and eat like everyone else. Even then, it's unrealistic to say that an army of mages and mortals got to Transilvania unnoticed, but the problems maximize a thousandfold when you move arround vampires.<br /> <br /> Plus, if the Tremere were to abandon their post, the vampires that were besieging them for a century would know and attack them in the back (that's what vampires do best).<br /> <br /> If this was a &quot;three sided war campaign&quot;, you can bet that some Gangrel and Nosferatu players would do all in their power to harass the Tremere army, while the Tzimisce Koldun player prepares his Ghouls for a hostile takeover. Meanwhile, the Mage players are likely getting loaded with rituals, and wont enter the Chantry without scanning for magical traps to counterspell (that's the only realistic approach if you enter in the palace of a mage).<br /> <br /> Meanwhile the Tremere, that now owes two thousand favors to all the Princes of the cities he had to stop by to get blood for two hundred monsters, needs to use his (probably weakened) army to battle against old Masters in their own warded Chantry protected by forcefields, dragons, and whatnot. Even if the Tremere win, the Masters are likely to be able to escape, and then the Tremere wouldn't be able to go back to their own place. Good luck establishing your new home before the Masters decide to take it back or nuke it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What i haven't found is a way for the younger mages to deal with larger numbers of supernatural opponents. </div> </div> </div>Mages can share their magick. When fighting against large numbers, you use magick on your surroundings or to empower humans (that are likely to be more numerous than the enemy). Order of Hermes would never lend a potent Artifact to a mortal, but to a young mage?, sure. Acolites are Hermetic too (1rst rank, mortal hermetics but hermetics nevertheless), and thus they can be trusted with a little magick. And nobody bans to use temporal enhacements even on mercenary troops.<br /> <br /> Probably Masters aren't going to get into the fray, instead sending his apprentices with powerful Artifacts and enhancing them with spells. Primus makes artifact creation easier than normal for Hermetics, thus, a &quot;sunlight orb&quot; or a &quot;volcanoe potion&quot; could be detonated by any young apprentice in the correct place. Golems and powerfulk elementals can be summoned to assist younger mages. Masters of Corona can walk the minds of their students and cast spells from that connection. With just the mental images received by Corona 3, a Master could already start a long range ritual against an enemy base, as per the range rules<br /> <br /> Young mages may have between 1 and 3 on one Pillar. Unlike Ascencion, in DA a mage can have more points on a Pillar than their Foundation, but there's a penalty as you need to spend Quintessence to cast a spell greater than your &quot;Arete&quot;. However, this does amplify what a starting mage could be expected to do.<br /> Merely enhancing the weapons of all the human soldiers with Aggravated damage (Primus 2) it's something that Apprentices can do, a group of archers with enchanted arrows can be quite deadly against vampires (bonus points if you set them on fire, just for the rotschreck). A warrior with enhanced weaponry and armor it's rival to any ghoul. They can then concentrate on countering spells and disciplines. Apprentices can control the emotions of both enemies and alies, making them great leaders as they can control the flow of battle. Others can heal, improve the bodies of their comerades, and use the nature against their enemies (you could take some cues of the Life thread for these Disciples). And, finally, Vires Disciples can redirect any energy or cinetical attack used against the platoon (a wind gust to repel lances and arows?), use ilusions (that can be countered by Auspex but only the Tremere themselves have that advantage), communicate orders trough the battlefield without anyone noticing, blast down walls and doors, start fires that make the undead check for rotschreck, and do great damage to key enemies.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm currently designing mages of varying levels of power and capability and vampires and gargoyles and such... and reading up on their capabilities. This may take a while. Th numbers on each side though will have great bearing on the tactics they can use. </div> </div> </div>Don't neglect DA: Grimoire...DA magick system makes no sense without reading that (and even then it's hard to understand the reasoning).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-23-2017, 05:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T11:40:40-07:00","id":1083114,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1082725#post1082725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Blood Treachery.<br /> <br /> As mark puts it in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/379957-how-anyone-tried-out-a-hermetic-tremere-war\">the old Thread that theorizes about just the kind of stuff you're thinking right now</a>:<br /> <br /> (you should read the whole rant, it's rather funny)</div> </div> </div> </div>Sounds like a plan.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1082725#post1082725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My point there it's not to proove that it's impossible for the Tremere to have been hidden for 100 years to the Order as a whole (I know that all of the above can be averted), but to highlight that, in all those years, it's unrealisticto think NOTHING got wrong. NO Tremere fell to the Beast in a bad moment, no spy was sucessful, no one of those mages that were fighting in the beginings of the Omen War was savvy enough to search for vampires in their midst.<br /> I'm more willing to believe that the &quot;masquerade had his accidents&quot;. A group of the Tremere did know, but decided not to tell, thinking that the corruption wouldn't be so big as to reach Ceoris, while others knew, but hadn't enough proof to call a tribunal, or didn't wanted to get caught in the middle of a war(and thus decided to lay low and avoid capture). I think that at least a chapter about this in some book would be appreciated. Otherwise mages seem just sitting ducks</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, my understanding of the Order knowing or not knowing about the Vampires was of the Order as a whole recognizing that Tremeres were becoming a Vampire group. So when I read that the house Tremere kept it hidden for 100+ years I don't read it as nothing ever went wrong. Just that not enough went wrong enough to alert anyone of enough not in the Order of Hermes to bring it to the attention of the Order as a whole. Maybe some people suspected such, but couldn't really convince others to check because they sounded like some weird entrapment scheme to catch people spying on fellow members of the Order. Maybe a Tremere Vampire showed up at the doorstep of a chantry and the guy who went to the Tremere to double check got brainwashed and the Vampire informant subsequently executed. Maybe people of the Order thought the Tremere had a weird vampire problem, not knowing how bad it was, and were waiting for the Tremere elders to ask for help, which they other Houses planned to give in exchange for favors. I don't recall the book saying absolutely no person in the Order knew or suspected anything, just that the Order (I take it as the order as a whole) found out too late.<br /> <br /> I know no one devoted a chapter or such to it and I suppose I see what you mean, but I personally don't take that as things having happened a certain way so much as the writers dumping the job of filling in the blanks on the Storyteller... which isn't all that great either but not quite the same thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1082725#post1082725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Any Tremere old enough to consent being killed to become immortal should be savvy (and, likely, powerful) enough to at least research what would happen.</div> </div> </div> </div>How would you research what would happen to a powerful mage who drinks the vampire formula?<br /> Also, what would you research from if the powerful mage who turned into a vampire made a book about what happens when a powerful mage turns into a Vampire?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1082725#post1082725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And doing all of that it's literally a lot easier than solving fermat's last theorem?, writing 1 Game of Thrones book takes more than developing the first Thaumaturgical grimoire?. Inventing Thaumaturgy itself it's easier than getting a phd?. Then how long takes to create a Path?, one month?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think you quite got my argument.<br /> <br /> It's more like the mathematical equations being already developed but missing a few crucial variables now that you are substituting Awakened Avatar or your Gnosis (Hermetic concept of Gnosis) empowered will for whatever the heck vampires draw their power from. Once you find the values of that variable or maybe other relevant variables, you don't have to reinvent the physics or the math, just recompute for everything and make your new tables and charts for the less brilliant students. Now someone without these tables and equations and notes would have to rediscover calculus and physics from scratch.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1082725#post1082725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You should get into the setting. This wasn't two castles in nearby mountains. The Order of Hermes was spread trough Europe. Meanwhile, the Tremere had become more insular. The Tremere were besieged by the Tzimisce, Gangrel, Nosferatu, and remaining Salubri.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, this occurred to me last time and is slowing down my presuppositions as I have this huge map now and am calculating traveling distances with that Blood magic used for increased travel speed for units. Also figuring out the needed populations to sustain these. Likely though, when they went to other places, they didn't really care about sustainable populations so much as immediate need, except in certain areas.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1082725#post1082725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In order to do what you imagine, the Tremere should have moved armies of blood sucking undead trough Europe. This it's a logistical nightmare, how are you going to provide the blood for the whole army and at the same time not attract the attention?, how are you going to move arround massive numbers of vampires trough the Domain of the undead Princes without making all the vampires in the world declare war at you?, how are you going to do all that unnoticed by other supernatural forces that hunt vampires?.</div> </div> </div> </div>A logistical nightmare indeed. I'm doing the computing.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1082725#post1082725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Plus, if the Tremere were to abandon their post, the vampires that were besieging them for a century would know and attack them in the back (that's what vampires do best).</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, I'd rather do this step by step with an accurate step 1 first, with yours and everyone's inputs.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1082725#post1082725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If this was a &quot;three sided war campaign&quot;, you can bet that some Gangrel and Nosferatu players would do all in their power to harass the Tremere army, while the Tzimisce Koldun player prepares his Ghouls for a hostile takeover. Meanwhile, the Mage players are likely getting loaded with rituals, and wont enter the Chantry without scanning for magical traps to counterspell (that's the only realistic approach if you enter in the palace of a mage).</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't worry, the I'm taking that into account too... eventually.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1082725#post1082725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Meanwhile the Tremere, that now owes two thousand favors to all the Princes of the cities he had to stop by to get blood for two hundred monsters, needs to use his (probably weakened) army to battle against old Masters in their own warded Chantry protected by forcefields, dragons, and whatnot. Even if the Tremere win, the Masters are likely to be able to escape, and then the Tremere wouldn't be able to go back to their own place. Good luck establishing your new home before the Masters decide to take it back or nuke it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm pretty sure the Tremere tried something other than talking to princes and owing them favors. I'll get to that. As for the latter thing... that's likely what happened. The whole thing probably devolved into a bizarre hide and seek all over Europe... but again, we'll have to get to that.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1077627-how-could-the-tremere-manage-to-win-the-first-massasa-war?p=1082725#post1082725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mages can share their magick. .... Don't neglect DA: Grimoire...DA magick system makes no sense without reading that (and even then it's hard to understand the reasoning).</div> </div> </div> </div>Thus i haven't quite made my next post that I intended to yet. I'm working on Mage Stats, Vampire Stats, Use of powers. Movement logistics. Stuff.<br /> <br /> <br /> Say, can someone maybe clarify the Vampire and Gargoyle and Mage numbers mentioned by Kendaan?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12480,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","user":"Spacecat","date":". 04-24-2017, 02:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T12:33:38-07:00","id":1083137,"title":"","content":"Well, looking foward to it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Say, can someone maybe clarify the Vampire and Gargoyle and Mage numbers mentioned by Kendaan?</div> </div> </div> </div>While I can't, we have the official numbers of the OoH at the time from Ars Magica, provided by Posthumanity (1230 - 92 Tremere = 1138 mages. A few more or less). Why don't use these and work upwards for vampires with a highball of 10 vampires per mage to a lowball of 3 vampires per mage (1: 1000000 mages vs 1:100000 vampires / 1: 150000 mages vs 1: 50000 vampires). Even when these censuses can't be trusted and are of the modern days I think it's rational to have a difference between mages and vampires that more or less refflect these numbers because while embrace was a bit more common back then, so were awakenings in a world that was more filled with magick. Also, the Tremere were notoriously few compared with other vampiric clans, they werent allowed to embrace a lot outside of Transilvania, and inside Transilvania the Tzimisce hold most of the Domains during the first century of the Omen War, and quite a lot after that 1rst century. Meanwhile, the OoH was the largest group of mages, so I would suggest the lowball, that seems to be the ratio you choosed (this it's the rationale I would use to justify that ratio).<br /> <br /> It seems that the Tremere (very cautiously) organized a &quot;diaspora&quot; of sorts, making Chantries outside Transilvania. These Chantries are not to fight in the war, but to survive in case they loose. Their success it's limmited by the fact that nobody likes Tremere, and Princes get sure to limmit the number of embraces they make. They're very small. Merlilinda's own chantry in Britain it's described as &quot;cramped&quot;, and that they don't have prisons or anything of the like, just rooms and labs. They won't fight, but could be used to ease the travel by preparing blood beforehand for the troops that need it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T16:20:12-07:00","id":1083217,"title":"","content":"Anyway, just to get this down, that's 92 Tremere vs 1138 mages of the rest of the Order<br /> <br /> With regards to the mages, these would be mages of the 10 remaining founding houses with very roughly 114 per house with very roughly 1 Arch-mage, 4 Masters, 20 Disciples, &amp; 89 Initiates<br /> In a hundred years, each house would have 1 Arch-mage, 8 Masters, 40 Disciples, &amp; 178 Initiates for a total of 227 per house, and a total of 2270 mages. Each House would probably be supported by people around 4540 people, which would be probably 1 relatively big town (the towns of the era usually having 2500 people but ranging from 1000 to 8000 people).<br /> <br /> Tremere's 92 would be divided into Tremere, The books suggest that the tremere ranks would be something like 1 Tremere, 7 Masters, 49 Disciples, and 35 Initiates to start with. Assuming a Transylvanian population of 520,896 we would have a maximum of 26044 Vampires before things become unsustainable. I'm guessing Tremere probably rocked the boat of the Vampire population with one third of that, or 8681 vampires in Transylvania, Including Gargoyles. This would give us possibly something like 1 Tremere, 7 Pseudo Elders, 49 Pseudo Ancillae, 343 Neonates, 2401 Fledgelings for a total of 2801 Tremere Vampires. If you want to incorporate some minority of mages, it'll probably be something like 1 Master, 7 Disciples, and 14 Initiates.<br /> <br /> Whatever the case, you'd also have 5880 Gargoyles.<br /> <br /> I'm trying to figure out how to incorporate the numbers of the other clans and figure out their numbers as well. Doing a bit of research, it seems the Elder Tzimisce were slow to act and tended to throw their progeny (including revenants) at problems until these were solved. They were also limited in what they could do by the machinations of the Ventrue. The Gangrel, were probably considerably more determined foes and were helped along by the Nosferatu.<br /> <br /> There is mention of a 1 to 1000 average ratio for vampires, and a strong suggestion that the Tzimisce went above this number. I'm ignoring this value with regards to the Tremere because this is supposedly the time they weren't acting like Vampires.<br /> <br /> <br /> On other notes, I'm assuming the first Step in the War involves a group of mages and whatever forces could be mustered by the nearby houses marching to conquer the Tremere lands and the Tremere attempting to wipe them out. I'm Still studying Vampires. For now, I think this will be a mostly Order Vs. Tremere affair, with the Gangrel jumping in if they can capitalize on the situation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12480,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","user":"Spacecat","date":". 04-24-2017, 09:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-25T08:32:03-07:00","id":1083469,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There is mention of a 1 to 1000 average ratio for vampires. . I'm ignoring this value </div> </div> </div>If we go by these numbers we would have 521+ vampires for Transylvania :P (little more if you &quot;take&quot; from nearby countries).<br /> I think that your numbers are more like it, maybe a bit on the big side, but not bad.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-26T08:40:16-07:00","id":1083986,"title":"","content":"Current Update [Edit: retroactive terminology change, also added a dot to &quot;Master&quot; pillars]<br /> <br /> I've been designing the mages who will take part in the conflict.<br /> They're looking something like this for Order of Hermes<br /> <br /> Archmage (of Forces) [Remove]<br /> Strength 5, Dexterity 5, Stamina 5,<br /> Charisma 5, Manipulation 5, Appearance 2<br /> Perception 5, Intelligence 5, Wits 5<br /> <br /> Awareness 5, Dodge 2, Empathy 2, Intimidation 2, Leadership 3, Subterfuge 3<br /> Archery 4, Crafts 4, Melee 4, Ride 2, Stealth 5, Survival 2<br /> Academics 2, Cosmology 2, Enigmas 3, Investigation 4, Medicine 2, Occult 5, Politics 3<br /> <br /> Willpower 10<br /> Forma 5, Anima 4, Corona 4, Primus 5, Vires 5<br /> Chantry 1, Fount 5, Library 5, Resources 4, Sanctum 4, Servant 3, Talisman 5<br /> <br /> <br /> Archmage (of Life) [Master of Life]<br /> Strength 5, Dexterity 5, Stamina 5,<br /> Charisma 5, Manipulation 5, Appearance 2<br /> Perception 5, Intelligence 5, Wits 5<br /> <br /> Awareness 5, Dodge 2, Empathy 2, Intimidation 2, Leadership 3, Subterfuge 3<br /> Archery 4, Crafts 4, Melee 4, Ride 2, Stealth 5, Survival 2<br /> Academics 2, Cosmology 2, Enigmas 3, Investigation 4, Medicine 2, Occult 5, Politics 3<br /> <br /> Willpower 10<br /> Forma 5, Anima 5, Corona 4, Primus 4, Vires 5<br /> Chantry 1, Fount 5, Library 5, Resources 4, Sanctum 4, Servant 3, Talisman 5<br /> <br /> <br /> Archmage (of Magic) [Master of Magic]<br /> Strength 5, Dexterity 5, Stamina 5,<br /> Charisma 5, Manipulation 5, Appearance 2<br /> Perception 5, Intelligence 5, Wits 5<br /> <br /> Awareness 5, Dodge 2, Empathy 2, Intimidation 2, Leadership 3, Subterfuge 3<br /> Archery 4, Crafts 4, Melee 4, Ride 2, Stealth 5, Survival 2<br /> Academics 2, Cos